"71_cocoahelp.mst — CocoaHelp v2: the Documentation browser for the native
 Cocoa GUI. A CocoaDocCrate-style reader: a topic SIDEBAR (NSTableView) on
 the left, a find-in-page field top right, and a styled document view whose
 markdown gets REAL typography — coloured headings with hairline rules,
 monospaced code blocks, callout quotes — not the flattened bold-only
 rendering v1 shipped. All documentation lives IN THIS FILE as Smalltalk
 methods, so the app documents itself from any working directory (v1 read
 docs/macvm_help.md relative to the process cwd, which broke under any
 launcher that was not run-cocoa.sh).

 Rendering: block-structural markdown is parsed HERE (headings, fenced
 code, quotes, rules — a small line-based splitter that keeps fences
 intact), and each block is styled through the two legacy attributed-string
 keys this bridge has already proven end to end: 'NSColor' (v1 used it to
 force labelColor) and 'NSFont' (the same legacy key family — the modern
 NSFontAttributeName IS the string 'NSFont'). Body paragraphs and lists
 still go through AppKit's own CommonMark parser (NSAttributedString
 initWithMarkdownString:..., the v1 pipeline), so **bold**, *italic*,
 `code spans` and bullet lists arrive styled for free; each parsed run is
 then forced to labelColor so dark mode reads (the v1 fix, kept per-block).

 Find: a plain NSTextField (Enter = find next, wraps). Matching is a
 case-insensitive scan done in Smalltalk over the RENDERED text (read back
 from the text storage once per topic, so offsets match what is displayed);
 every match gets a translucent systemYellow background attribute, and the
 current one is selected + scrolled visible. Content stays in the Basic
 Multilingual Plane so Smalltalk character indices equal NSString UTF-16
 offsets (the range math depends on it — no emoji in the docs).

 Self-registers by name like every CG7+ view; CocoaUI startup now OPENS on
 this view (the Welcome topic) instead of an empty Workspace."
"I am the Documentation tab: a sidebar of topics, a find field, and a
 styled markdown reader. My topics — the manifesto, history and credits,
 a Smalltalk language primer, tours of the environment and the world, and
 build-your-own guides — are written in me as methods, so the system
 documents itself wherever it runs. I run in the UI worker VM."
Object subclass: CocoaHelp [
    <classVars: Topics Sections SidebarHandles SidebarHandleList CodeBlocks Container Sidebar SidebarScroll SidebarFace SidebarDelegate
                DocView DocScroll FindField RunButton RunButtons RunDelegates ActionDelegates
                PendingUiSrc UiRunDelegate
                CurrentTopic DocText FindTerm Matches MatchCursor
                BodyFont BodyBold BodyItalic BodyCode>

    "── registration (CG7+ pattern; CocoaUI startup calls this by name) ──"
    CocoaHelp class >> register [
        CocoaUI registerViewNamed: #help title: 'Docs' icon: 'documentation'
                container: [ self containerView ]
                onShow: [ self ensureSelection ]
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> containerView [
        Container isNil ifTrue: [ self buildView ].
        ^Container
    ]

    "── the topic registry: (title, content selector) pairs, sidebar order ──"
    "── the documentation TREE ────────────────────────────────────────────
     Sections, each holding articles. The sidebar is an NSOutlineView over
     this, so a section can grow to a dozen articles without the flat list
     becoming a wall of titles — which is the point: there is a great deal
     still to write and it needs somewhere to go.

     Built with add:, NOT `Array with:...` — that family tops out at four
     arguments in this world, and the fifth silently becomes a DNU."
    CocoaHelp class >> sections [
        | s |
        Sections isNil ifFalse: [ ^Sections ].
        s := OrderedCollection new.
        s add: (self section: 'Getting Started' articles: (OrderedCollection new
            add: (Array with: 'Welcome'             with: #welcomeText);
            add: (Array with: 'The Manifesto'       with: #manifestoText);
            add: (Array with: 'History and Credits' with: #historyText);
            yourself)).
        s add: (self section: 'The Language' articles: (OrderedCollection new
            add: (Array with: 'The Smalltalk Language' with: #languageText);
            add: (Array with: 'The Strongtalk World'   with: #worldText);
            add: (Array with: 'Exceptions'             with: #exceptionsText);
            add: (Array with: 'Strings and Text'       with: #stringsText);
            add: (Array with: 'Workers and VMs'        with: #workersText);
            yourself)).
        s add: (self section: 'The Environment' articles: (OrderedCollection new
            add: (Array with: 'The Environment'       with: #environmentText);
            add: (Array with: 'The Monitor'           with: #monitorText);
            add: (Array with: 'Caring for Your World' with: #worldCareText);
            yourself)).
        s add: (self section: 'Guides' articles: (OrderedCollection new
            add: (Array with: 'Build an App'   with: #appGuideText);
            add: (Array with: 'Build a Game'   with: #gameGuideText);
            add: (Array with: 'Edit Sprites and Sounds' with: #assetEditorsText);
            add: (Array with: 'A Little Weather App' with: #weatherGuideText);
            add: (Array with: 'Test Your Code' with: #testGuideText);
            add: (Array with: 'Automation'     with: #automationText);
            yourself)).
        s add: (self section: 'Reference' articles: (OrderedCollection new
            add: (Array with: 'Collections'     with: #collectionsText);
            add: (Array with: 'Numbers'         with: #numbersText);
            add: (Array with: 'Dates and Times' with: #datesText);
            add: (Array with: 'Sockets'         with: #socketsText);
            add: (Array with: 'Under the Hood'  with: #internalsText);
            yourself)).
        Sections := s asArray.
        ^Sections
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> section: title articles: anOrderedCollection [
        ^Array with: title with: anOrderedCollection asArray
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> sidebarView [ "scripting/tests" ^Sidebar ]
    CocoaHelp class >> sectionCount [ ^self sections size ]
    CocoaHelp class >> sectionTitleAt: i [ ^(self sections at: i) at: 1 ]
    CocoaHelp class >> articlesOfSection: i [ ^(self sections at: i) at: 2 ]

    "The FLAT spine, DERIVED from the tree. showTopic:, the find bar and the
     Run buttons all address articles by a single index and keep doing so —
     the tree changed how the sidebar PRESENTS articles, not how the rest of
     the class addresses them."
    CocoaHelp class >> topics [
        | t |
        Topics isNil ifFalse: [ ^Topics ].
        t := OrderedCollection new.
        self sections do: [ :sec | (sec at: 2) do: [ :art | t add: art ] ].
        Topics := t asArray.
        ^Topics
    ]

    "The two directions the outline needs to talk to the flat spine."
    CocoaHelp class >> flatIndexOfSection: si article: ai [
        | n |
        n := 0.
        1 to: si - 1 do: [ :k | n := n + (self articlesOfSection: k) size ].
        ^n + ai
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> sectionAndArticleOfFlat: flat [
        | n |
        n := 0.
        1 to: self sectionCount do: [ :si |
            | count |
            count := (self articlesOfSection: si) size.
            flat <= (n + count) ifTrue: [ ^Array with: si with: flat - n ].
            n := n + count ].
        ^Array with: 1 with: 1
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> topicCount [ ^self topics size ]
    CocoaHelp class >> nl [ ^String with: Character nl ]
    CocoaHelp class >> topicTitleAt: i [ ^(self topics at: i) at: 1 ]

    "── build ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"
    CocoaHelp class >> sidebarWidth [ ^222.0 ]
    CocoaHelp class >> findBarHeight [ ^40.0 ]

    CocoaHelp class >> buildView [
        | frame w h |
        frame := CocoaUI contentHost onMain frame.
        w := frame at: 3. h := frame at: 4.
        ActionDelegates := OrderedCollection new.
        Container := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSView') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithFrame: (Array with: 0.0 with: 0.0 with: w with: h).
        Container onMain setAutoresizesSubviews: true.
        self buildSidebar: h.
        self buildFindField: w height: h.
        self buildDocView: w height: h.
        self showTopic: 1.
        self selectSidebarArticle: 1.
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> buildSidebar: h [
        | col |
        SidebarScroll := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSScrollView') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithFrame: (Array with: 0.0 with: 0.0 with: self sidebarWidth with: h).
        SidebarScroll onMain setHasVerticalScroller: true.
        SidebarScroll onMain setAutoresizingMask: CocoaUI maskHeightSizable.
        SidebarScroll onMain setBorderType: 0.
        "An OUTLINE, not a table: the index is a tree of sections now, and
         NSOutlineView is the widget that already knows how to disclose one.
         Everything else about the sidebar — source-list style, adaptive
         fill, row height — is unchanged."
        Sidebar := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSOutlineView') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithFrame: (Array with: 0.0 with: 0.0 with: self sidebarWidth with: h).
        col := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSTableColumn') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithIdentifier: 'topic'.
        col onMain setWidth: 600.0.
        Sidebar onMain addTableColumn: col.
        "An outline needs to be TOLD which column carries the disclosure
         triangle; without this it renders as a flat list with no way to
         expand anything."
        Sidebar onMain setOutlineTableColumn: col.
        col release.
        Sidebar onMain setHeaderView: nil.
        Sidebar onMain setRowHeight: 28.0.
        "Rounded source-list style selection, the modern sidebar look
         (NSTableViewStyleSourceList = 2; harmlessly ignored pre-Big Sur)."
        Sidebar onMain setStyle: 2.
        Sidebar onMain setColumnAutoresizingStyle: 1.
        "underPageBackgroundColor is the adaptive 'behind the content' tone —
         the standard sidebar fill, and it tracks light/dark by itself."
        Sidebar onMain setBackgroundColor:
            ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor') onMain underPageBackgroundColor).
        SidebarScroll onMain setDrawsBackground: false.
        SidebarFace := (Worker classNamed: #CocoaHelpTopicFace) new.
        SidebarDelegate := MacvmDelegate outlineSourceOn: SidebarFace.
        Sidebar onMain setDataSource: SidebarDelegate.
        Sidebar onMain setDelegate: SidebarDelegate.
        SidebarScroll onMain setDocumentView: Sidebar.
        Container onMain addSubview: SidebarScroll.
        self deferReload: Sidebar.
        self expandAllSections.
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> buildFindField: w height: h [
        | x |
        x := self sidebarWidth + 8.
        FindField := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSTextField') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithFrame: (Array with: x with: h - 32.0
                                  with: w - x - self runButtonWidth - 24.0 with: 24.0).
        FindField onMain setPlaceholderString: 'Find in page  (press Return; wraps)'.
        FindField onMain setBezelStyle: 1. "rounded — search-field look"
        FindField onMain setAutoresizingMask: CocoaUI maskWidthSizable + CocoaUI maskMinYMargin.
        self wireAction: FindField to: [ self runFind ].
        Container onMain addSubview: FindField.
        self buildRunButton: w height: h.
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> runButtonWidth [ ^168.0 ]

    CocoaHelp class >> buildRunButton: w height: h [
        "Every guide here is code you are meant to RUN. This lifts the
         listing your caret is sitting in straight into the Workspace and
         does it — no select, no copy, no tab hunting."
        | btn |
        btn := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSButton') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithFrame: (Array with: w - self runButtonWidth - 10.0
                                  with: h - 32.0
                                  with: self runButtonWidth with: 24.0).
        btn onMain setTitle: 'Run in Workspace'.
        btn onMain setBezelStyle: 1.
        btn onMain setAutoresizingMask: CocoaUI maskMinXMargin + CocoaUI maskMinYMargin.
        self wireAction: btn to: [ self runInWorkspace ].
        RunButton := btn.
        Container onMain addSubview: btn.
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> clearRunButtons [
        RunButtons isNil ifFalse: [
            RunButtons do: [ :b |
                b isValid ifTrue: [ b onMain removeFromSuperview. b release ] ] ].
        RunButtons := OrderedCollection new.
        RunDelegates := OrderedCollection new.
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> placeRunButtons [
        "A Run button beside EVERY listing: these guides exist to be tried,
         and 'select, copy, find the Workspace, paste' is four steps too
         many. Positioned from the real text layout — the layout manager's
         bounding rect for the block's rendered range, offset by the text
         container origin — and added as subviews of the TEXT VIEW, so they
         scroll with the code they belong to."
        | lm tc origin ox oy vw |
        self clearRunButtons.
        (CodeBlocks isNil or: [ CodeBlocks isEmpty ]) ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        lm := DocView onMain layoutManager.
        tc := DocView onMain textContainer.
        "Text layout is LAZY — without this the rects come back empty for
         anything below the first screenful."
        lm onMain ensureLayoutForTextContainer: tc.
        origin := DocView onMain textContainerOrigin.
        ox := origin at: 1. oy := origin at: 2.
        vw := (DocView onMain frame) at: 3.
        CodeBlocks do: [ :entry |
            | start len src rect x y btn |
            start := entry at: 1. len := entry at: 2. src := entry at: 3.
            (len > 0 and: [ (entry at: 4) ~~ #ref ]) ifTrue: [
                rect := lm onMain
                    boundingRectForGlyphRange: (Array with: start with: len)
                    inTextContainer: tc.
                x := ox + (rect at: 1) + (rect at: 3) + 12.
                x := x min: vw - 46.0.
                y := oy + (rect at: 2).
                btn := self runButtonFor: src at: x y: y kind: (entry at: 4).
                DocView onMain addSubview: btn.
                RunButtons add: btn ] ].
        lm release. tc release.
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> runButtonFor: src at: x y: y kind: aKind [
        | btn holder delegate img launch |
        launch := aKind == #launch.
        btn := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSButton') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithFrame: (Array with: x with: y with: 34.0 with: 20.0).
        img := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSImage') onMain
            imageWithSystemSymbolName: (launch
                ifTrue: [ 'play.rectangle.fill' ]
                ifFalse: [ aKind == #ui ifTrue: [ 'play.square.fill' ]
                                        ifFalse: [ 'play.fill' ] ])
            accessibilityDescription: 'Run this example'.
        img isNil
            ifTrue: [ btn onMain setTitle: 'Run' ]
            ifFalse: [ btn onMain setImage: img. btn onMain setTitle: '' ].
        btn onMain setBezelStyle: 1.
        btn onMain setToolTip: (launch
            ifTrue: [ 'Launch this as a program (its own window)' ]
            ifFalse: [ aKind == #ui
                ifTrue: [ 'Run this here, in the interface VM' ]
                ifFalse: [ 'Run this example in the Workspace' ] ]).
        btn onMain setAutoresizingMask: 0.
        holder := CocoaToolbarAction on: [
            aKind == #launch ifTrue: [ self launch: src ] ifFalse: [
                aKind == #ui ifTrue: [ self runHere: src ]
                             ifFalse: [ self run: src ] ] ].
        delegate := MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn: holder.
        btn onMain setTarget: delegate.
        btn onMain setAction: 'macvmAction:'.
        RunDelegates add: delegate.
        ^btn
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> run: src [
        "SWITCH FIRST — views build lazily on first visit and the app opens
         on Docs, so on a fresh launch the Workspace does not exist yet and
         setWorkspaceText: (which guards on a nil Workspace) would silently
         drop the code."
        CocoaUI switchToView: #workspace.
        CocoaUI setWorkspaceText: src.
        CocoaUI doIt.
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> trimTrailingWhitespace: aString [
        "A fenced block ends with a newline; `launchDemo:` wants the bare
         entry doit."
        | j sp lf tab |
        sp := Character value: 32. lf := Character value: 10. tab := Character value: 9.
        j := aString size.
        [ j >= 1 and: [ (aString at: j) = sp or: [ (aString at: j) = lf
                        or: [ (aString at: j) = tab ] ] ] ] whileTrue: [ j := j - 1 ].
        ^aString copyFrom: 1 to: j
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> runHere: src [
        "Evaluate in THIS VM — the interface worker. View code (`CocoaUI`,
         `Cocoa classNamed:`, `MacvmDelegate`) exists only here; the Workspace
         runs on the primary, where those names are undeclared, so a Workspace
         run of a view example fails with `undeclared variable 'CocoaUI'`.

         DEFERRED to the next run-loop turn, not run inline: the evaluated
         source almost always performs `onMain` sends (that is what view code
         IS), and a main-thread send issued from inside a handler that is
         itself holding the main thread fails with 'no main run loop?'.
         Handing it to the run loop puts it in an ordinary main-thread turn
         where those sends complete."
        PendingUiSrc := src.
        UiRunDelegate isNil ifTrue: [
            UiRunDelegate := MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn:
                (CocoaToolbarAction on: [ self runPendingUi ]) ].
        UiRunDelegate onMain performSelector: 'macvmAction:'
                      withObject: nil afterDelay: 0.0
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> runPendingUi [
        | src r |
        src := PendingUiSrc.
        PendingUiSrc := nil.
        src isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        r := [ Worker primEvalDoit: src ]
                on: Error do: [ :e | 'error: ' , e messageText printString ].
        CocoaUI appendTranscript: 'Docs (interface VM) => ' , r printString.
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> launch: src [
        "Start `src` as a TOP-LEVEL program on the primary, through the host
         service — the same door the Demos menu uses. A game's frame loop must
         own its activation; started from a Workspace doit (a nested one) it
         never gets a window at all. THIS view runs in the UI worker, so it can
         reach the host service that the Workspace cannot."
        | bc entry |
        entry := self trimTrailingWhitespace: src.
        bc := Worker classNamed: #CocoaBrowser.
        bc isNil ifTrue: [
            ^CocoaUI appendTranscript: 'Docs: no host service — cannot launch.' ].
        bc hostService onMain launchDemo: entry.
        CocoaUI appendTranscript: 'Docs: launched ' , entry , '  (Escape closes it)'.
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> probeRectFor: start len: len [
        "Geometry probe: the on-screen rect of a character range."
        | lm tc |
        lm := DocView onMain layoutManager.
        tc := DocView onMain textContainer.
        ^lm onMain boundingRectForGlyphRange: (Array with: start with: len)
                   inTextContainer: tc
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> probeOrigin [ ^DocView onMain textContainerOrigin ]
    CocoaHelp class >> codeBlockCount [ ^CodeBlocks isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ CodeBlocks size ] ]
    CocoaHelp class >> codeBlockAt: i [ ^CodeBlocks at: i ]

    CocoaHelp class >> codeAtCaret [
        "The listing the caret sits in, else the one just above it, else the
         topic's first — so the button always has something to run on a page
         that has any code at all. nil when the topic has none."
        | caret best |
        (CodeBlocks isNil or: [ CodeBlocks isEmpty ]) ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        caret := (DocView onMain selectedRange) at: 1.
        best := nil.
        CodeBlocks do: [ :pair |
            (pair at: 1) <= caret ifTrue: [ best := pair ] ].
        best isNil ifTrue: [ best := CodeBlocks first ].
        ^best at: 3
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> runInWorkspace [
        "Selection wins if there is one (you can run a single line of a
         listing); otherwise the whole block at the caret."
        | sel src range |
        range := DocView onMain selectedRange.
        src := nil.
        (range at: 2) > 0 ifTrue: [
            sel := (DocView onMain string) onMain
                       substringWithRange: range.
            sel isNil ifFalse: [ src := sel asString. sel release ] ].
        src isNil ifTrue: [ src := self codeAtCaret ].
        src isNil ifTrue: [ ^CocoaUI appendTranscript: 'Docs: no code on this page to run.' ].
        self run: src.
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> buildDocView: w height: h [
        | x dw tc |
        x := self sidebarWidth + 1.
        dw := w - x.
        DocView := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSTextView') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithFrame: (Array with: 0.0 with: 0.0 with: dw with: h - self findBarHeight).
        DocView onMain setEditable: false.
        DocView onMain setSelectable: true.
        DocView onMain setVerticallyResizable: true.
        DocView onMain setHorizontallyResizable: false.
        DocView onMain setAutoresizingMask: CocoaUI maskWidthSizable.
        "Generous margins — the single cheapest 'this is a document, not a
         terminal' signal (NSSize as a 2-array, the same 16-byte-struct
         marshal scrollRangeToVisible:'s NSRange already uses)."
        DocView onMain setTextContainerInset: (Array with: 26.0 with: 20.0).
        tc := DocView onMain textContainer.
        tc isNil ifFalse: [
            tc onMain setWidthTracksTextView: true.
            tc release ].
        DocScroll := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSScrollView') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithFrame: (Array with: x with: 0.0 with: dw with: h - self findBarHeight).
        DocScroll onMain setHasVerticalScroller: true.
        DocScroll onMain setAutoresizingMask: CocoaUI maskWidthSizable + CocoaUI maskHeightSizable.
        DocScroll onMain setBorderType: 0.
        DocScroll onMain setDocumentView: DocView.
        Container onMain addSubview: DocScroll.
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> wireAction: aControl to: aBlock [
        | holder delegate |
        holder := CocoaToolbarAction on: aBlock.
        delegate := MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn: holder.
        aControl onMain setTarget: delegate.
        aControl onMain setAction: 'macvmAction:'.
        ActionDelegates add: delegate.
    ]

    "── sidebar plumbing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────"
    "── outline handles ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
     NSOutlineView hands items back as opaque ids, so each node is an
     INTERNED NSString carrying its path: 's' for section s, 's.a' for its
     article a. Interning matters — the outline compares items by identity
     when it tracks expansion, so minting a fresh NSString per callback
     would make every section forget it was open. Same discipline as
     CocoaOutliner's handleFor:."
    CocoaHelp class >> handleFor: path [
        | k h |
        SidebarHandles isNil ifTrue: [
            SidebarHandles := Dictionary new. SidebarHandleList := OrderedCollection new ].
        k := path asSymbol.
        h := SidebarHandles at: k ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        h isNil ifTrue: [
            h := Cocoa nsString: path.
            SidebarHandles at: k put: h.
            SidebarHandleList add: h ].
        ^h
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> pathOf: item [
        ^item isNil ifTrue: [ '' ] ifFalse: [ item sendString: 'description' ]
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> releaseSidebarHandles [
        SidebarHandleList isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        SidebarHandleList do: [ :h | h isValid ifTrue: [ h release ] ].
        SidebarHandles := nil. SidebarHandleList := nil.
    ]

    "── the outline data source ──────────────────────────────────────────"

    CocoaHelp class >> outlineChildCountOf: item [
        | path parts |
        path := self pathOf: item.
        path isEmpty ifTrue: [ ^self sectionCount ].
        parts := path splitOn: $..
        "a section has articles; an article has none"
        parts size = 1 ifTrue: [ ^(self articlesOfSection: (parts at: 1) asInteger) size ].
        ^0
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> outlineChild: i of: item [
        | path |
        path := self pathOf: item.
        path isEmpty ifTrue: [ ^self handleFor: (i + 1) printString ].
        ^self handleFor: path , '.' , (i + 1) printString
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> outlineTitleOf: item [
        | path parts |
        path := self pathOf: item.
        path isEmpty ifTrue: [ ^'' ].
        parts := path splitOn: $..
        parts size = 1 ifTrue: [ ^self sectionTitleAt: (parts at: 1) asInteger ].
        ^((self articlesOfSection: (parts at: 1) asInteger)
              at: (parts at: 2) asInteger) at: 1
    ]
    "The flat article index for a selected row, or 0 for a section header —
     which is what lets the selection handler ignore section clicks."
    CocoaHelp class >> flatIndexOf: item [
        | path parts |
        path := self pathOf: item.
        path isEmpty ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        parts := path splitOn: $..
        parts size = 1 ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        ^self flatIndexOfSection: (parts at: 1) asInteger
              article: (parts at: 2) asInteger
    ]

    "Open every section. Done by expanding each section ITEM rather than the
     nil-item 'expand everything' form, because that one needs
     expandItem:expandChildren: (two arguments, so it cannot ride
     performSelector:withObject:) and, more importantly, an outline answers
     its data source on demand — expanding a known item works without
     waiting for a reload to have populated any rows.

     Sections start OPEN: a tree whose sections are all shut hides the whole
     index behind five triangles, which is worse than the flat list it
     replaced. Collapsing is the user's to choose."
    CocoaHelp class >> expandAllSections [
        Sidebar isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        1 to: self sectionCount do: [ :i |
            Sidebar onMain expandItem: (self handleFor: i printString) ]
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> deferReload: aView [
        "Never reload a table synchronously from a callback (the CocoaBrowser
         rule): post it for the next run-loop turn."
        aView isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        aView onMain performSelector: 'reloadData' withObject: nil afterDelay: 0.0
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> ensureSelection [
        "Runs on EVERY switch to this view. The reload is the point: an
         NSTableView fills itself from its data source when it first DRAWS,
         so a sidebar built while its tab was off-screen — or rebuilt after
         a UI-worker restart — can come up empty and stay that way, since
         nothing else ever asks it to refresh. Reload on every show, then
         re-assert the highlight (a reload drops the selection)."
        Container isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        CurrentTopic isNil ifTrue: [ self showTopic: 1 ].
        self deferReload: Sidebar.
        self expandAllSections.
        self selectSidebarArticle: CurrentTopic.
    ]

    "Select the row showing a FLAT article index. A row is no longer the
     article's own number — section headers occupy rows too — so the article
     is turned back into its tree item and the outline asked where that item
     currently sits. Expanding first matters: rowForItem: answers -1 for an
     item inside a collapsed section."
    CocoaHelp class >> selectSidebarArticle: flat [
        | sa item row |
        Sidebar isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        (flat isNil or: [ flat < 1 ]) ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        self expandAllSections.
        sa := self sectionAndArticleOfFlat: flat.
        item := self handleFor: (sa at: 1) printString , '.' , (sa at: 2) printString.
        row := Sidebar send: 'rowForItem:' args: (Array with: item) ret: #i64.
        row < 0 ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        Sidebar onMain selectRowIndexes: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSIndexSet') onMain
                                              indexSetWithIndex: row)
                byExtendingSelection: false
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> topicSelected [
        "A row is now either a section header or an article. Selecting a
         header selects no article — it just toggles the disclosure — so a
         flat index of 0 means 'nothing to show', not 'show the first'."
        | row item flat |
        Sidebar isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        row := Sidebar onMain selectedRow.
        row < 0 ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        item := Sidebar onMain itemAtRow: row.
        item isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        flat := self flatIndexOf: item.
        flat = 0 ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        (CurrentTopic isNil or: [ CurrentTopic ~= flat ])
            ifTrue: [ self showTopic: flat ]
    ]

    "── text scaling (real point sizes; the A- / A+ toolbar buttons reach here)
     Every doc font is built at BASE + `CocoaUI fontDelta` — the SAME delta the
     Workspace/Editor/Browser use — so one control scales the whole app, and
     changing it simply re-renders the page (reflowForFontChange). The body is
     styled BY HAND (appendStyled:) rather than by AppKit''s CommonMark parser,
     because the parser bakes fixed sizes into runs we cannot reach from
     Smalltalk to resize. This is plain text at a larger size — it reflows in
     the same column. It is emphatically NOT scroll-view magnification, which
     (with fullscreen) froze the whole Mac."
    CocoaHelp class >> docDelta [ ^CocoaUI fontDelta ]
    CocoaHelp class >> sysFontOfSize: base [
        ^(Cocoa classNamed: 'NSFont') onMain systemFontOfSize: ((base + self docDelta) max: 6.0)
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> boldFontOfSize: base [
        ^(Cocoa classNamed: 'NSFont') onMain boldSystemFontOfSize: ((base + self docDelta) max: 6.0)
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> italicFontOfSize: base [
        | fm plain result |
        fm := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSFontManager') onMain sharedFontManager.
        plain := self sysFontOfSize: base.
        result := fm onMain convertFont: plain toHaveTrait: 1.  "NSItalicFontMask"
        plain release. fm release.
        ^result
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> refreshBodyFonts [
        "Cache the four inline body fonts at the current delta (released and
         rebuilt each render) so appendStyled: reuses them across every run
         instead of minting a wrapper per run."
        BodyFont isNil ifFalse: [ BodyFont release. BodyFont := nil ].
        BodyBold isNil ifFalse: [ BodyBold release. BodyBold := nil ].
        BodyItalic isNil ifFalse: [ BodyItalic release. BodyItalic := nil ].
        BodyCode isNil ifFalse: [ BodyCode release. BodyCode := nil ].
        BodyFont := self sysFontOfSize: 13.0.
        BodyBold := self boldFontOfSize: 13.0.
        BodyItalic := self italicFontOfSize: 13.0.
        BodyCode := CocoaUI fixedFontOfSize: 12.0.
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> flattenSoftBreaks: aString [
        "CommonMark soft break: a single newline inside a paragraph is a space,
         so the paragraph reflows. Match that before styling."
        | ws lf |
        lf := Character value: 10.
        ws := WriteStream on: String new.
        aString do: [ :c | ws nextPut: (c = lf ifTrue: [ $  ] ifFalse: [ c ]) ].
        ^ws contents
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> closeEmph: marker in: s from: start [
        "1-based index of the closing `*`/`**` whose preceding char is non-space
         (right-flanking), searching from `start`; 0 if none. The flanking rule
         keeps a stray literal `*` from opening/closing emphasis."
        | n k dbl |
        n := s size. dbl := marker size = 2. k := start.
        [ k <= n ] whileTrue: [
            ((s at: k) = $* and: [ k > start and: [ (s at: k - 1) ~= $  ] ]) ifTrue: [
                dbl
                    ifTrue: [ (k < n and: [ (s at: k + 1) = $* ]) ifTrue: [ ^k ] ]
                    ifFalse: [ ^k ] ].
            k := k + 1 ].
        ^0
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> appendStyled: aString into: storage [
        "Render inline **bold**, *italic* and `code` by hand into `storage`,
         each run in its delta-scaled body font (BodyFont/BodyBold/BodyItalic/
         BodyCode, cached by refreshBodyFonts). `code` is literal inside."
        | s i n color plainStart flush |
        s := self flattenSoftBreaks: aString.
        n := s size.
        color := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor') onMain labelColor.
        i := 1. plainStart := 1.
        flush := [ :endIx |
            endIx >= plainStart ifTrue: [
                self append: (s copyFrom: plainStart to: endIx)
                     font: BodyFont color: color into: storage ] ].
        [ i <= n ] whileTrue: [
            | c handled |
            c := s at: i. handled := false.
            c = $` ifTrue: [
                | j |
                j := i + 1.
                [ j <= n and: [ (s at: j) ~= $` ] ] whileTrue: [ j := j + 1 ].
                j <= n ifTrue: [
                    flush value: i - 1.
                    self append: (s copyFrom: i + 1 to: j - 1)
                         font: BodyCode color: color into: storage.
                    i := j + 1. plainStart := i. handled := true ] ].
            (handled not and: [ c = $* ]) ifTrue: [
                | dbl mstart mend |
                dbl := (i < n) and: [ (s at: i + 1) = $* ].
                mstart := dbl ifTrue: [ i + 2 ] ifFalse: [ i + 1 ].
                (mstart <= n and: [ (s at: mstart) ~= $  ]) ifTrue: [
                    mend := self closeEmph: (dbl ifTrue: [ '**' ] ifFalse: [ '*' ]) in: s from: mstart.
                    mend = 0 ifFalse: [
                        flush value: i - 1.
                        self append: (s copyFrom: mstart to: mend - 1)
                             font: (dbl ifTrue: [ BodyBold ] ifFalse: [ BodyItalic ])
                             color: color into: storage.
                        i := dbl ifTrue: [ mend + 2 ] ifFalse: [ mend + 1 ].
                        plainStart := i. handled := true ] ] ].
            handled ifFalse: [ i := i + 1 ] ].
        flush value: n.
    ]

    "── rendering ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"
    CocoaHelp class >> reflowForFontChange [
        "A+/A- changed the shared delta: re-render the current topic at the new
         size (every font is read fresh from `CocoaUI fontDelta`). No zoom, no
         magnification — just a normal re-render."
        (Container isNil or: [ CurrentTopic isNil ]) ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        self showTopic: CurrentTopic.
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> showTopic: i [
        | md storage empty ns |
        CurrentTopic := i.
        FindTerm := nil. Matches := nil. MatchCursor := 0.
        self refreshBodyFonts.
        md := self perform: ((self topics at: i) at: 2).
        storage := DocView onMain textStorage.
        empty := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSAttributedString') onMain alloc) onMain init.
        storage onMain setAttributedString: empty.
        empty release.
        CodeBlocks := OrderedCollection new.
        storage onMain beginEditing.
        (self blocksOf: md) do: [ :b | self renderBlock: b into: storage ].
        storage onMain endEditing.
        "One read-back so find offsets match exactly what is displayed."
        ns := storage onMain string.
        DocText := ns asString.
        ns release.
        storage release.
        DocView onMain scrollRangeToVisible: (Array with: 0 with: 0).
        "AND again next run-loop turn: the text was replaced a moment ago and
         AppKit lays it out lazily, so an immediate scroll measures against
         the OLD layout and leaves the new topic's title clipped above the
         top edge. Same deferral rule as the table reloads."
        DocView onMain performSelector: 'scrollToBeginningOfDocument:'
                withObject: nil afterDelay: 0.0.
        self placeRunButtons.
        "`i` is the FLAT article index, not a row — the outline turns it back
         into a row itself (selectSidebarArticle:)."
        self selectSidebarArticle: i.
    ]

    "A block is (tag, text): #h1/#h2/#h3 (heading text), #code (verbatim
     lines), #quote (bar-stripped lines), #rule, or #body (markdown source
     for AppKit's parser — paragraphs and bullet lists ride together so the
     parser lays the list out itself)."
    CocoaHelp class >> blocksOf: md [
        | lines blocks buf mode lf flushBody fenceKind |
        lf := Character value: 10.
        lines := self linesOf: md.
        blocks := OrderedCollection new.
        buf := WriteStream on: String new.
        mode := #none.
        fenceKind := #code.
        flushBody := [
            buf contents isEmpty ifFalse: [
                blocks add: (Array with: #body with: buf contents) ].
            buf := WriteStream on: String new.
            mode := #none ].
        lines do: [ :line |
            (mode = #code)
                ifTrue: [
                    (self isFence: line)
                        ifTrue: [
                            blocks add: (Array with: fenceKind with: buf contents).
                            buf := WriteStream on: String new.
                            mode := #none ]
                        ifFalse: [
                            buf contents isEmpty ifFalse: [ buf nextPut: lf ].
                            buf nextPutAll: line ] ]
                ifFalse: [
                    (self isFence: line) ifTrue: [
                        flushBody value.
                        fenceKind := #code.
                        (self fenceInfoOf: line) = 'launch' ifTrue: [ fenceKind := #launch ].
                        (self fenceInfoOf: line) = 'ref' ifTrue: [ fenceKind := #ref ].
                        (self fenceInfoOf: line) = 'ui' ifTrue: [ fenceKind := #ui ].
                        mode := #code ]
                    ifFalse: [ (self headingLevel: line) > 0 ifTrue: [
                        flushBody value.
                        blocks add: (Array
                            with: (self headingTagFor: (self headingLevel: line))
                            with: (self headingTextOf: line)) ]
                    ifFalse: [ (self isRule: line) ifTrue: [
                        flushBody value.
                        blocks add: (Array with: #rule with: '') ]
                    ifFalse: [ (self isBullet: line) ifTrue: [
                        flushBody value.
                        blocks add: (Array with: #bullet with: (self bulletTextOf: line)) ]
                    ifFalse: [ (self isTableRow: line) ifTrue: [
                        mode = #table ifFalse: [ flushBody value. mode := #table ].
                        buf contents isEmpty ifFalse: [ buf nextPut: lf ].
                        buf nextPutAll: line ]
                    ifFalse: [ (self isQuote: line) ifTrue: [
                        mode = #quote ifFalse: [ flushBody value. mode := #quote ].
                        buf contents isEmpty ifFalse: [ buf nextPut: lf ].
                        buf nextPutAll: (self quoteTextOf: line) ]
                    ifFalse: [ line isEmpty
                        ifTrue: [
                            mode = #quote
                                ifTrue: [
                                    blocks add: (Array with: #quote with: buf contents).
                                    buf := WriteStream on: String new.
                                    mode := #none ]
                                ifFalse: [
                                    mode = #table
                                        ifTrue: [
                                            blocks add: (Array with: #table with: buf contents).
                                            buf := WriteStream on: String new.
                                            mode := #none ]
                                        ifFalse: [ flushBody value ] ] ]
                        ifFalse: [
                            mode = #quote ifTrue: [
                                blocks add: (Array with: #quote with: buf contents).
                                buf := WriteStream on: String new.
                                mode := #none ].
                            mode = #table ifTrue: [
                                blocks add: (Array with: #table with: buf contents).
                                buf := WriteStream on: String new.
                                mode := #none ].
                            buf contents isEmpty ifFalse: [ buf nextPut: lf ].
                            buf nextPutAll: line.
                            mode := #body ] ] ] ] ] ] ] ] ].
        mode = #code ifTrue: [ blocks add: (Array with: fenceKind with: buf contents) ].
        mode = #quote ifTrue: [ blocks add: (Array with: #quote with: buf contents) ].
        mode = #table ifTrue: [ blocks add: (Array with: #table with: buf contents) ].
        mode = #body ifTrue: [ flushBody value ].
        ^blocks
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> linesOf: aString [
        | lf out buf |
        lf := Character value: 10.
        out := OrderedCollection new.
        buf := WriteStream on: String new.
        aString do: [ :ch |
            ch = lf
                ifTrue: [ out add: buf contents. buf := WriteStream on: String new ]
                ifFalse: [ buf nextPut: ch ] ].
        out add: buf contents.
        ^out
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> isFence: line [
        ^line size >= 3 and: [ (line at: 1) = $` and: [ (line at: 2) = $` and: [ (line at: 3) = $` ] ] ]
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> fenceInfoOf: line [
        "The word after the opening fence. ```launch marks a block that must
         start TOP-LEVEL through the host service (a game's frame loop cannot
         live inside a Workspace doit) rather than run in the Workspace."
        ^line size <= 3 ifTrue: [ '' ] ifFalse: [ line copyFrom: 4 to: line size ]
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> isRule: line [
        "Three or more dashes and NOTHING else — otherwise a '--- thought'
         aside, or worse a '- item' bullet, would vanish into a rule."
        | ok |
        line size < 3 ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        ok := true.
        line do: [ :ch | ch = $- ifFalse: [ ok := false ] ].
        ^ok
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> isBullet: line [
        "A '- ' or '* ' list item. Handled as its OWN block: AppKit's markdown
         parser drops every inter-block separator, so several bullets inside
         one parsed string arrive as one run-on paragraph (the same finding
         v1 recorded for headings). Owning the line breaks here is the fix."
        | sp |
        sp := Character value: 32.
        ^line size >= 2
            and: [ ((line at: 1) = $- or: [ (line at: 1) = $* ])
            and: [ (line at: 2) = sp ] ]
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> bulletTextOf: line [
        ^line copyFrom: 3 to: line size
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> isQuote: line [
        ^line size >= 1 and: [ (line at: 1) = $> ]
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> quoteTextOf: line [
        | i |
        i := 2.
        (line size >= 2 and: [ (line at: 2) = (Character value: 32) ]) ifTrue: [ i := 3 ].
        ^line size < i ifTrue: [ '' ] ifFalse: [ line copyFrom: i to: line size ]
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> headingLevel: line [
        | i sp |
        sp := Character value: 32.
        i := 1.
        [ i <= line size and: [ (line at: i) = $# ] ] whileTrue: [ i := i + 1 ].
        (i - 1 between: 1 and: 3) ifFalse: [ ^0 ].
        (i <= line size and: [ (line at: i) = sp ]) ifFalse: [ ^0 ].
        ^i - 1
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> headingTagFor: lvl [
        lvl = 1 ifTrue: [ ^#h1 ].
        lvl = 2 ifTrue: [ ^#h2 ].
        ^#h3
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> headingTextOf: line [
        | lvl |
        lvl := self headingLevel: line.
        ^line copyFrom: lvl + 2 to: line size
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> renderBlock: pair into: storage [
        | tag text |
        tag := pair at: 1. text := pair at: 2.
        tag = #h1 ifTrue: [
            self append: text , self nl
                 font: (self boldFontOfSize: 27.0)
                 color: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor') onMain systemGreenColor)
                 into: storage.
            ^self appendRuleInto: storage ].
        tag = #h2 ifTrue: [
            self append: self nl , text , self nl
                 font: (self boldFontOfSize: 19.0)
                 color: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor') onMain systemBlueColor)
                 into: storage.
            ^self appendRuleInto: storage ].
        tag = #h3 ifTrue: [
            ^self append: self nl , text , self nl , self nl
                 font: (self boldFontOfSize: 15.0)
                 color: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor') onMain systemTealColor)
                 into: storage ].
        tag = #bullet ifTrue: [
            | start |
            start := storage onMain length.
            self append: '-   '
                 font: (self sysFontOfSize: 13.0)
                 color: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor') onMain systemBlueColor)
                 into: storage.
            self appendInline: text into: storage.
            self append: self nl font: nil color: nil into: storage.
            ^self indentFrom: start in: storage first: 22.0 head: 42.0 ].
        tag = #rule ifTrue: [ ^self appendRuleInto: storage ].
        tag = #table ifTrue: [ ^self appendTable: text into: storage ].
        (tag = #code or: [ tag = #launch or: [ tag = #ref or: [ tag = #ui ] ] ]) ifTrue: [
            | start |
            start := storage onMain length.
            "Remember where this listing landed and what it says, so the
             Run in Workspace button can find the block the caret is in."
            CodeBlocks add: (Array with: start with: 0 with: text with: tag).
            self append: (self padded: text) , self nl
                 font: (CocoaUI fixedFontOfSize: 12.0)
                 color: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor') onMain systemPurpleColor)
                 into: storage.
            self indentFrom: start in: storage first: 22.0 head: 22.0.
            "A faint panel behind the listing — the strongest 'this is code'
             signal after the monospace itself. quaternaryLabelColor is the
             lightest adaptive fill AppKit ships, so it stays subtle in both
             appearances."
            self tintFrom: start in: storage
                 color: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor') onMain quaternaryLabelColor).
            "Rendered length — padding makes it longer than the raw source,
             and the run-button geometry measures the RENDERED range."
            CodeBlocks last at: 2 put: (storage onMain length) - start.
            ^self append: self nl font: nil color: nil into: storage ].
        tag = #quote ifTrue: [
            | start |
            self append: self nl font: nil color: nil into: storage.
            start := storage onMain length.
            self append: (self barPrefixed: text) , self nl
                 font: (self sysFontOfSize: 13.0)
                 color: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor') onMain systemOrangeColor)
                 into: storage.
            self indentFrom: start in: storage first: 18.0 head: 40.0.
            ^self append: self nl font: nil color: nil into: storage ].
        self appendMarkdown: text into: storage.
    ]

    "── tables (GFM pipe tables) ──────────────────────────────────────────
     AppKit's CommonMark parser has NO tables — they are a GFM extension —
     so a `| a | b |` line reaches it as literal pipes and renders as
     literal pipes. Parsed and laid out here instead: cells split on the
     bars, the |---|---| separator row dropped, every column padded to its
     widest cell, and the grid set MONOSPACED so the columns actually line
     up. A proportional font would undo the padding."

    CocoaHelp class >> isTableRow: aLine [
        ^(aLine trimSeparators) startsWith: '|'
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> cellsOf: aLine [
        "'| a | b |' -> ('a' 'b'). The leading and trailing bars are frame,
         not separators, so they are stripped before splitting."
        | t out |
        t := aLine trimSeparators.
        (t startsWith: '|') ifTrue: [ t := t copyFrom: 2 to: t size ].
        (t endsWith: '|') ifTrue: [ t := t copyFrom: 1 to: t size - 1 ].
        out := OrderedCollection new.
        (t subStrings: '|') do: [ :c | out add: c trimSeparators ].
        ^out asArray
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> isSeparatorCells: cells [
        "The |---|:--:| row: every cell is dashes and colons, and there is
         at least one dash. It carries alignment in GFM; here it only marks
         where the header ends."
        | any |
        any := false.
        cells do: [ :c |
            c isEmpty ifTrue: [ ^false ].
            c do: [ :ch |
                (ch = $- or: [ ch = $: ]) ifFalse: [ ^false ].
                ch = $- ifTrue: [ any := true ] ] ].
        ^any
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> padTo: n text: aString [
        | out |
        out := WriteStream on: String new.
        out nextPutAll: aString.
        (aString size + 1) to: n do: [ :i | out nextPut: $  ].
        ^out contents
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> appendTable: text into: storage [
        "Header in teal with a hairline rule beneath it, body rows in the
         ordinary label colour, the whole grid on the same faint panel a
         code listing gets — so a table reads as a figure, not as prose."
        | rows widths header start |
        rows := OrderedCollection new.
        (self linesOf: text) do: [ :line |
            | cells |
            line trimSeparators isEmpty ifFalse: [
                cells := self cellsOf: line.
                (self isSeparatorCells: cells) ifFalse: [ rows add: cells ] ] ].
        rows isEmpty ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        "Widest cell per column, over every row."
        widths := OrderedCollection new.
        rows do: [ :cells |
            1 to: cells size do: [ :i |
                widths size < i ifTrue: [ widths add: 0 ].
                (widths at: i) < (cells at: i) size
                    ifTrue: [ widths at: i put: (cells at: i) size ] ] ].
        self append: self nl font: nil color: nil into: storage.
        start := storage onMain length.
        header := true.
        rows do: [ :cells |
            | line |
            line := WriteStream on: String new.
            line nextPutAll: '  '.
            1 to: cells size do: [ :i |
                line nextPutAll: (self padTo: (widths at: i) text: (cells at: i)).
                i < cells size ifTrue: [ line nextPutAll: '   ' ] ].
            self append: line contents , self nl
                 font: (CocoaUI fixedFontOfSize: 12.0)
                 color: (header
                            ifTrue: [ (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor') onMain systemTealColor ]
                            ifFalse: [ (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor') onMain labelColor ])
                 into: storage.
            header ifTrue: [
                "A rule of the same width as the widest row, drawn in the
                 header colour, so the head separates without a border."
                | ruleWidth rule |
                ruleWidth := 0.
                widths do: [ :w | ruleWidth := ruleWidth + w + 3 ].
                rule := WriteStream on: String new.
                rule nextPutAll: '  '.
                "A LITERAL '─', appended whole — not `Character value: 9472`.
                 A String here is a BYTE vector, so constructing a character
                 above 255 truncates it to one invalid byte and the bridge
                 then (correctly) refuses to hand Cocoa invalid UTF-8:
                 `Cocoa main-thread send failed ... 'appendString:'`. Written
                 as source text it is just its three UTF-8 bytes."
                1 to: ruleWidth - 3 do: [ :i | rule nextPutAll: '─' ].
                self append: rule contents , self nl
                     font: (CocoaUI fixedFontOfSize: 12.0)
                     color: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor') onMain tertiaryLabelColor)
                     into: storage.
                header := false ] ].
        self indentFrom: start in: storage first: 22.0 head: 22.0.
        self tintFrom: start in: storage
             color: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor') onMain quaternaryLabelColor).
        ^self append: self nl font: nil color: nil into: storage
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> indentFrom: start in: storage first: fi head: hi [
        "Hanging indent over the range appended since `start`. Without it a
         wrapped bullet or quote line falls back to the left margin and the
         list stops reading as a list. 'NSParagraphStyle' is the legacy
         attribute key, the same family as 'NSFont'/'NSColor' this file
         already relies on."
        | ps len |
        len := storage onMain length.
        len <= start ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        ps := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMutableParagraphStyle') onMain alloc) onMain init.
        ps onMain setFirstLineHeadIndent: fi.
        ps onMain setHeadIndent: hi.
        ps onMain setParagraphSpacing: 4.0.
        storage onMain addAttribute: 'NSParagraphStyle' value: ps
                range: (Array with: start with: len - start).
        ps release.
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> tintFrom: start in: storage color: aColor [
        | len |
        len := storage onMain length.
        len <= start ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        storage onMain addAttribute: 'NSBackgroundColor' value: aColor
                range: (Array with: start with: len - start).
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> padded: text [
        "Pad every code line to the block's longest, so the tint behind it is
         a RECTANGLE. A background attribute only paints the characters it
         covers, so unpadded lines leave a ragged right edge that reads as
         damage rather than as a panel."
        | lines w out first |
        lines := self linesOf: text.
        w := 0.
        lines do: [ :ln | w := w max: ln size ].
        w := w + 2.
        out := WriteStream on: String new.
        first := true.
        lines do: [ :ln | | pad |
            first ifTrue: [ first := false ] ifFalse: [ out nextPut: Character nl ].
            out nextPutAll: ln.
            pad := w - ln size.
            1 to: pad do: [ :i | out nextPut: (Character value: 32) ] ].
        ^out contents
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> barPrefixed: text [
        "Each quote line gets a leading bar, the callout look."
        | out lf |
        lf := Character value: 10.
        out := WriteStream on: String new.
        (self linesOf: text) do: [ :ln |
            out contents isEmpty ifFalse: [ out nextPut: lf ].
            out nextPutAll: '|   ' , ln ].
        ^out contents
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> appendRuleInto: storage [
        "A hairline: a run of BOX DRAWINGS LIGHT HORIZONTAL in the adaptive
         separator colour, small type so it stays thin."
        | rule |
        rule := WriteStream on: String new.
        "96 underscores (~480pt at 9pt) — a deliberate SHORT rule. A
         full-width one wraps to a second line the moment the window
         narrows, and two stacked bars read as a rendering fault."
        1 to: 96 do: [ :i | rule nextPut: $_ ].
        "Underscores draw a continuous baseline stroke. tertiaryLabelColor
         (not separatorColor — that is so light it read as nothing at all on
         the light theme) at 9pt: present, never loud, and it adapts."
        self append: rule contents , self nl
             font: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSFont') onMain systemFontOfSize: 9.0)
             color: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor') onMain tertiaryLabelColor)
             into: storage.
    ]

    "Append plain text and style THE WHOLE RUN with a font and/or colour.
     Range math uses storage lengths (UTF-16 units), never Smalltalk size —
     robust for every BMP character the docs use."
    CocoaHelp class >> append: aString font: aFont color: aColor into: storage [
        | start after ms |
        start := storage onMain length.
        ms := storage onMain mutableString.
        ms onMain appendString: aString.
        ms release.
        after := storage onMain length.
        after > start ifTrue: [
            | r |
            r := Array with: start with: after - start.
            "CLEAR the inheritable block attributes first. NSTextStorage gives
             freshly appended characters the attributes of the character
             before them, so a code block's tint and a bullet's hanging
             indent would otherwise bleed forward into every following
             heading and paragraph (seen exactly that way: a grey wash over
             the next heading and its rule). Each block therefore starts
             clean and opts back in — tintFrom:/indentFrom: run AFTER this."
            storage onMain removeAttribute: 'NSBackgroundColor' range: r.
            storage onMain removeAttribute: 'NSParagraphStyle' range: r.
            aFont isNil ifFalse: [
                storage onMain addAttribute: 'NSFont' value: aFont range: r ].
            aColor isNil ifFalse: [
                storage onMain addAttribute: 'NSColor' value: aColor range: r ] ].
    ]

    "Body block: rendered by hand (appendStyled:) rather than by the CommonMark
     parser, so every inline run is built in a delta-scaled font and the whole
     page grows/shrinks with the A+/A- buttons. Ends with a blank line."
    CocoaHelp class >> appendMarkdown: aString into: storage [
        | ms |
        self appendStyled: aString into: storage.
        ms := storage onMain mutableString.
        ms onMain appendString: self nl , self nl.
        ms release.
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> appendInline: aString into: storage [
        "appendMarkdown:'s sibling for a block whose line breaks WE own (a
         bullet): inline styling only, no trailing gap."
        self appendStyled: aString into: storage.
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> markdownOptions [
        | opts |
        opts := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSAttributedStringMarkdownParsingOptions') alloc init.
        opts setInterpretedSyntax: 0. "Full — emphasis, code spans, lists"
        opts setFailurePolicy: 1.     "ReturnPartiallyParsedIfPossible"
        ^opts
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> attributedFor: aString [
        | opts attr |
        opts := self markdownOptions.
        attr := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSAttributedString') alloc
            send: 'initWithMarkdownString:options:baseURL:error:'
            args: (Array with: (Cocoa nsString: aString) with: opts with: nil with: nil)
            ret: #id.
        opts release.
        ^attr
    ]

    "── find in page ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────"
    CocoaHelp class >> runFind [
        | ns term |
        (FindField isNil or: [ DocText isNil ]) ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        ns := FindField onMain stringValue.
        term := ns asString.
        ns release.
        term isEmpty ifTrue: [ ^self clearFind ].
        (FindTerm isNil or: [ FindTerm ~= term ])
            ifTrue: [
                FindTerm := term.
                Matches := self matchesOf: term in: DocText.
                MatchCursor := 0.
                self highlightMatches: term size ].
        Matches isEmpty ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        MatchCursor := MatchCursor \\ Matches size + 1.
        self jumpToMatch: (Matches at: MatchCursor) length: term size.
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> find: aString [
        "Scripting/automation door (the CocoaFind typeTerm: precedent): fill
         the field and run a find, as pressing Return would."
        FindField isNil ifFalse: [ FindField onMain setStringValue: aString ].
        self runFind
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> matchCount [ ^Matches isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ Matches size ] ]
    CocoaHelp class >> currentTopicIndex [ ^CurrentTopic ]
    CocoaHelp class >> sidebarSelectedRow [
        ^Sidebar isNil ifTrue: [ -2 ] ifFalse: [ Sidebar onMain selectedRow ]
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> clearFind [
        | storage len |
        FindTerm := nil. Matches := nil. MatchCursor := 0.
        DocView isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        storage := DocView onMain textStorage.
        len := storage onMain length.
        len > 0 ifTrue: [
            storage onMain removeAttribute: 'NSBackgroundColor'
                    range: (Array with: 0 with: len) ].
        storage release.
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> highlightMatches: termLen [
        | storage len bg |
        storage := DocView onMain textStorage.
        len := storage onMain length.
        len > 0 ifTrue: [
            storage onMain removeAttribute: 'NSBackgroundColor'
                    range: (Array with: 0 with: len) ].
        bg := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSColor') onMain systemYellowColor) onMain
                  colorWithAlphaComponent: 0.35.
        Matches do: [ :pos |
            storage onMain addAttribute: 'NSBackgroundColor' value: bg
                    range: (Array with: pos - 1 with: termLen) ].
        storage release.
    ]

    CocoaHelp class >> jumpToMatch: pos length: termLen [
        DocView onMain setSelectedRange: (Array with: pos - 1 with: termLen).
        DocView onMain scrollRangeToVisible: (Array with: pos - 1 with: termLen).
    ]

    "Case-insensitive substring scan, ASCII fold — answers 1-based starts."
    CocoaHelp class >> lowered: ch [
        | v |
        v := ch value.
        ^(v >= 65 and: [ v <= 90 ]) ifTrue: [ Character value: v + 32 ] ifFalse: [ ch ]
    ]
    CocoaHelp class >> matchesOf: term in: text [
        | out tn n i j ok |
        out := OrderedCollection new.
        tn := term size. n := text size.
        tn = 0 ifTrue: [ ^out ].
        i := 1.
        [ i <= (n - tn + 1) ] whileTrue: [
            j := 1. ok := true.
            [ ok and: [ j <= tn ] ] whileTrue: [
                (self lowered: (text at: i + j - 1)) = (self lowered: (term at: j))
                    ifFalse: [ ok := false ].
                j := j + 1 ].
            ok ifTrue: [ out add: i ].
            i := i + 1 ].
        ^out
    ]

    "── teardown (CG9 rebuild; CocoaUI class >> teardown calls by name) ──"
    CocoaHelp class >> teardown [
        "The interned outline handles are NSStrings this class owns; a UI
         rebuild must let them go or every teardown leaks one per node.
         Sections/Topics are dropped too, so a reopened Help rebuilds the
         tree rather than serving handles that point into a released outline."
        self releaseSidebarHandles.
        DocView isNil ifFalse: [ DocView isValid ifTrue: [ DocView release ] ].
        DocScroll isNil ifFalse: [ DocScroll isValid ifTrue: [ DocScroll release ] ].
        Sidebar isNil ifFalse: [ Sidebar isValid ifTrue: [ Sidebar release ] ].
        SidebarScroll isNil ifFalse: [ SidebarScroll isValid ifTrue: [ SidebarScroll release ] ].
        FindField isNil ifFalse: [ FindField isValid ifTrue: [ FindField release ] ].
        RunButton isNil ifFalse: [ RunButton isValid ifTrue: [ RunButton release ] ].
        Container isNil ifFalse: [ Container isValid ifTrue: [ Container release ] ].
        Container := nil. Sidebar := nil. SidebarScroll := nil. SidebarFace := nil.
        SidebarDelegate := nil. DocView := nil. DocScroll := nil. FindField := nil.
        ActionDelegates := nil. CurrentTopic := nil. DocText := nil.
        RunButton := nil. CodeBlocks := nil.
        RunButtons := nil. RunDelegates := nil.
        Sections := nil. Topics := nil.
        PendingUiSrc := nil. UiRunDelegate := nil.
        FindTerm := nil. Matches := nil. MatchCursor := 0.
    ]
]

"The sidebar's table data source / delegate receiver — the CocoaTableFace
 pattern: AppKit asks the minted MacvmDelegate, which performs these here."
"The sidebar's data source. An OUTLINE face now: the index is a tree of
 sections, and each of these five selectors is what NSOutlineView asks."
Object subclass: CocoaHelpTopicFace [
    outlineView: ov numberOfChildrenOfItem: item [ ^CocoaHelp outlineChildCountOf: item ]
    outlineView: ov isItemExpandable: item [ ^(CocoaHelp outlineChildCountOf: item) > 0 ]
    outlineView: ov child: i ofItem: item [ ^CocoaHelp outlineChild: i of: item ]
    outlineView: ov objectValueForTableColumn: col byItem: item [
        ^CocoaHelp outlineTitleOf: item ]
    outlineViewSelectionDidChange: aNote [ CocoaHelp topicSelected ]
]

"── the documentation itself ──────────────────────────────────────────────
 Each topic is one markdown string. The renderer above understands: #/##/###
 headings (green/blue/teal, hairline rules), ``` fenced code (monospaced,
 purple), > callout quotes (orange bar), --- rules, and body text through
 AppKit's CommonMark parser (**bold**, *italic*, `code spans`, - bullets).
 House rules: apostrophes doubled for Smalltalk, BMP characters only."
Object subclass: CocoaHelp [

    CocoaHelp class >> welcomeText [ ^'# Welcome to MACVM

MACVM is a **Smalltalk system**: a live world of objects you can inspect, change and extend while it runs — carried by a fast virtual machine written from scratch in Rust for Apple Silicon, in the spirit of *Strongtalk*.

Everything you see in this window is written in Smalltalk, running on that VM, including this documentation browser.

## Where to start

- Pick a topic from the **sidebar** on the left. *The Manifesto* says why this exists; *History and Credits* honours the shoulders this stands on.
- New to the language? Read **The Smalltalk Language** — twenty minutes, and the whole system opens up.
- Impatient? Open the **Workspace** from the toolbar, type `3 + 4 * 2`, and press **Cmd-P** (Print It). For a game, use the **Demos** menu — Breakout is a good first look.
- The field above this text **finds in the page** — type a word and press Return.

> Nothing here is read-only. Every tool in the toolbar — the Browser, the Editor, the games in the Demos menu — is Smalltalk source you can open, read, and change while it runs.

## The one-minute tour

The toolbar switches between views: the **Workspace** (a scratchpad for running code), two **Browsers** (the class library), the **Editor**, the **Outliner**, **Find**, the **Canvas**, this **Docs** view, and the **Debugger**. The **Transcript** — the system''s log — docks at the bottom of the window. The metrics at the right of the toolbar are the VM''s own live vital signs.

Welcome aboard. The system is yours.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> manifestoText [ ^'# The Manifesto

**Build a new Strongtalk for modern systems.**

In 1994 a small team built the fastest Smalltalk that has ever existed, and one of the most advanced virtual machines of any kind: Strongtalk. Type-feedback inlining, on-stack replacement, deoptimization — the ideas that later powered Java''s HotSpot and every serious JavaScript engine were proven *first* in a Smalltalk. Then the team was acquired, the work was shelved, and the world mostly forgot.

MACVM exists to continue that line, not merely to preserve it.

## What we believe

- **A live system beats a dead one.** You should be able to open any object, read any method, and change the running world — including the tools themselves. The system must never make you leave it to understand it.
- **Speed is respect.** A dynamic language does not have to be slow, and Strongtalk proved it three decades ago. MACVM measures itself honestly against the fastest Smalltalk VMs in existence, on the same benchmarks, on the same machine — and it holds its own.
- **Small enough to understand.** One person should be able to read the whole VM. No hundred-megabyte runtime, no build farm — a single Rust crate, a folder of Smalltalk source, and a Mac.
- **Correctness is a discipline, not a hope.** Every change to the compiler must produce *byte-identical* output between the interpreter and the JIT across the entire test suite, survive garbage-collection stress that scavenges at every allocation, and pay its way on the benchmarks. The gates are not optional.
- **Native, not hosted.** This is a real Mac app: AppKit windows, Metal graphics, AppleScript automation. The platform is embraced, not abstracted away.

## What this is not

Not a museum piece, not an emulator, and not a toy. It is a working system for building things — games, tools, experiments — that happens to carry one of computing''s great unfinished ideas forward on the hardware of today.

---

> The dream was always a system that is alive all the way down, and fast enough that you never have to apologise for it. That dream now runs on the machine in front of you.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> historyText [ ^'# History and Credits

MACVM stands on fifty years of work by people who believed computing could be personal, live, and beautiful. This page is their credit line.

## Smalltalk — Xerox PARC, 1972-1980

Smalltalk was born in Alan Kay''s Learning Research Group at Xerox PARC: **Alan Kay**''s vision of the Dynabook and of *objects all the way down*; **Dan Ingalls**, who implemented Smalltalk-72 through Smalltalk-80 and made the impossible look easy, over and over; **Adele Goldberg**, who led the books and the release that taught the world. Around them: Ted Kaehler, Diana Merry, Scott Wallace, Peter Deutsch and many others.

**L. Peter Deutsch** and **Allan Schiffman**''s 1984 paper on efficient Smalltalk implementation — dynamic translation, inline caches — is the founding document of every fast dynamic-language VM since, this one included.

## Self — the radical middle child

**David Ungar** and **Randall Smith**''s Self (1987-1995) pushed pure message passing to its limit and forced the VM breakthroughs that made it fast: **Craig Chambers**''s customization and maps, **Urs Hoelzle**''s polymorphic inline caches, type feedback and *dynamic deoptimization*. MACVM''s architecture — per-class compiled methods, PICs, deopt with frame materialization — is Self''s lineage, straight down.

## Strongtalk — Animorphic Systems, 1994-1997

The namesake. A tiny company — **David Griswold**, **Lars Bak**, **Gilad Bracha**, **Urs Hoelzle**, **Steffen Grarup**, **Robert Griesemer** and colleagues — built the fastest Smalltalk ever: a type-feedback inlining JIT over a clean blue-book image, plus (Bracha and Griswold) an *optional* static type system that never slowed the dynamic heart. Sun acquired the team in 1997; their VM became the **HotSpot** Java VM. Strongtalk itself was open-sourced in 2006, a gift to everyone who came after.

## The living lineage

- **Squeak** (1996) — Kay, Ingalls and team brought Smalltalk back as open source.
- **OpenSmalltalk / Cog** — **Eliot Miranda**''s JIT VM carries Squeak and **Pharo** today. Cog is one of the two reference points MACVM benchmarks against, with respect.
- **The Dart VM** — **Lars Bak** and **Kasper Lund**''s engine (2011). Its recognized-methods and intrinsics design directly informed MACVM''s primitive-fusion work; a sibling project runs this same Smalltalk dialect on a preserved Dart 1.24 VM.

## MACVM

Designed and built in 2026 by **Alban Read**, in daily collaboration with **Claude** (Anthropic) as pair programmer — a from-scratch Rust VM for Apple Silicon, its Smalltalk world written file by file, its every optimization gated by tests and honest benchmarks. A long-held dream, made real together.

> To all the builders above: thank you. May your work stay interesting, and may it never be forgotten.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> languageText [ ^'# The Smalltalk Language

Smalltalk fits on a postcard: **everything is an object, and all you can do is send messages**. Even `3 + 4` is a message. Even `ifTrue:` is a message. There is no other mechanism.

## Literals

```
42.  -17.  3.14159.       "numbers (integers of any size)"
$a.  $?.                  "characters"
''hello world''.            "strings (double the quote to escape)"
#name.  #at:put:.         "symbols — interned names"
#(1 2 3).                 "a literal array"
true.  false.  nil        "the usual suspects"
```

## Three kinds of message

**Unary** — just a name, tightest binding:

```
5 factorial.         "120"
''macvm'' size.        "5"
3.7 rounded          "4"
```

**Binary** — operator-shaped, middle binding:

```
3 + 4 * 2.           "14 — strictly left to right! (3+4)*2"
10 // 3.             "3, integer division"
''mac'' , ''vm''         ", concatenates: ''macvm''"
```

**Keyword** — the colons carry the arguments, loosest binding:

```
| d array |
d := Dictionary new.
array := #(10 20 30 40 50).
d at: #speed put: 88.
7 between: 1 and: 10.
array copyFrom: 2 to: 5
```

So `d at: 1 + 2 put: 3 factorial` means `d at: 3 put: 6`. Parentheses do the rest.

## Blocks — code as objects

A block is a closure in square brackets. Blocks make Smalltalk''s control flow *library code*, not syntax:

```
| count total n limit |
count := 3. total := 0. n := 1. limit := 40.
[ :x | x * x ] value: 9.                       "81"
count > 0 ifTrue: [ Transcript showCr: ''go'' ].
1 to: 5 do: [ :i | total := total + i ].
[ n < limit ] whileTrue: [ n := n * 2 ].
#(3 1 2) collect: [ :e | e * 10 ]              "(30 10 20)"
```

## Cascades and statements

Statements end with a period. A semicolon **cascades** — more messages to the same receiver:

```
Transcript showCr: ''one''; showCr: ''two''; showCr: ''three''.
```

## Defining a class

This world uses a compact file syntax — here is a real, complete class:

```
Object subclass: Counter [
    | count |
    Counter class >> new [ ^super new setZero ]
    setZero [ count := 0 ]
    increment [ count := count + 1. ^count ]
    count [ ^count ]
]
```

`| count |` declares instance variables; `Counter class >> new` defines a class-side method; `^` returns. Class variables are declared with a pragma: `<classVars: Registry NextId>`.

## Exceptions

Full ANSI-style exceptions, implemented in the world itself:

```
| n |
[ 10 / 0 ] on: ZeroDivide do: [ :e | Transcript showCr: e messageText ].
[ 100 sqrt ] ensure: [ Transcript showCr: ''ensured — this always runs'' ].
n := 0.
[ n := n + 1. n < 3 ifTrue: [ Error signal ]. n ]
    on: Error do: [ :e | e retry ]
```

Handlers can `return:`, `retry`, `pass`, and `resume:` — the whole protocol.

> Try every one of these RIGHT NOW: copy a line into the Workspace and press Cmd-P. The best way to learn Smalltalk is to ask the system.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> environmentText [ ^'# The Environment

One window, many views — the toolbar switches between them, and everything shares the live world underneath.

## Workspace

The scratchpad. Type any expression, then:

- **Cmd-D** — *Do It*: run the selection (or the whole workspace).
- **Cmd-P** — *Print It*: run it and print the result right there.

The Workspace is where every experiment starts: `TestRunner start`, `1000 factorial`, `Object subclass: ...`. (Games are the exception — they need a top-level launch, see *Guide: Build a Game*.)

## Transcript

The system log, docked at the bottom of the window (drag the divider; it remembers). `Transcript showCr: ''hello''` writes to it from anywhere — worker output is forwarded here too.

## The Browsers

Two ways into the class library. **Browser** is the direct pane: classes, variables, selectors, source. **Browser V2** is the classic Smalltalk-80 four-pane category browser — categories, classes, protocols, methods — with full write access: accept a method edit and it compiles into the running world *and* persists.

## Editor, Outliner, Find

The **Editor** is a plain class-text editor with live recompile on save. The **Outliner** is the Strongtalk-style unfolding tree over live class reflection. **Find** searches selectors and source across the whole world.

## Canvas, Sprite Editor, Sound Editor

The **Canvas** is an RGBA drawing surface driven from Smalltalk. Under the **Tools** menu: the **Sprite Editor** (draw GamePane sprites, save them as sheet classes) and the **Sound Editor** (the ADSR panel over the parametric synthesizer).

## Debugger

When code halts — a breakpoint, an error with halt-on-error enabled, an explicit `halt` — the **Debugger** fronts itself with the stack, the source, and the frame; Step Into, Step Over, Finish, Continue, Abort.

## Appearance and type

**View - Appearance**: System, Light or Dark. **A-** and **A+** in the toolbar scale every code surface together.

## Demos

The **Demos** menu carries the show-offs: Breakout, MandelZoom, ParallelMandel, Worms, Galaxigans and friends — each one an ordinary Smalltalk class you can read in the Browser while it runs. The menu launches them **top-level on the primary** (`CocoaBrowser hostService onMain launchDemo:`), which is what a frame loop needs; **Escape** ends one.

## Automation

The app speaks **AppleScript**, so scripts — and AI agents through `osascript` — can drive it:

```ref
tell application id "com.macvm.cocoa"
    evaluate "6 * 7"
end tell
```

They can run code, switch views, snapshot the window, and read state. See the **Automation** page for the whole story.

> The metrics cluster at the toolbar''s right edge is the VM itself: memory in use, GC activity, compiled-code counts — live, always on.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> worldText [ ^'# The Strongtalk World

The *world* is the Smalltalk side of MACVM: a folder of plain text files, loaded in order at startup, that build everything from `Object` up to this documentation browser.

## Files, in order

`world/world.list` names the files and the order. Numbering tells the story: kernel classes first (`01_object.mst`, `04a_blockclosure.mst`, `06_smallinteger.mst`...), then collections, streams, numbers — then the fun (`43_gamepane.mst`, `44_breakout.mst`) — then the tools. The Cocoa GUI layer has its own list (`world/cocoaui.list`) so headless runs never load AppKit code.

A `.mst` file is just class definitions and expressions. Top-level expressions run *at load time* — the world literally executes itself into existence.

## The library

Everything you expect from a Smalltalk-80 image, implemented in readable source: `Collection` protocols over `Array`, `OrderedCollection`, `Dictionary`, `Set`, `Bag`, `SortedCollection`; `Stream`s; arbitrary-precision `LargeInteger` arithmetic; `Date` and `Time`; exceptions; and the reflection the browsers are built on.

## Tests

The world tests itself:

```
TestRunner start.
TestRunner run: IntegerTests.
```

The full suite — over **7,700 assertions** — runs in both the interpreter and the JIT, and the two transcripts must match *byte for byte*. That differential gate is the project''s spine: the JIT is not allowed to disagree with the interpreter, ever.

## The image

Source files are the truth, but edits made in the browsers persist to a versioned SQLite image (`image.sqlite3`) alongside the world — accept a method in the Browser and it is compiled into the running system *and* recorded durably.

## Benchmarks

`BenchmarkDashboard` drives the classic suite — richards, deltablue, and the micro set — with the same workloads and the same clocks used to compare against Cog and the Dart VM. The numbers on the project''s wall came from here.

> Read the world the way you would read a good book: start at 01_object.mst and just keep going. There is no magic underneath — it is Smalltalk all the way down until you hit the VM.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> exceptionsText [ ^'# Exceptions

Errors in macVM are **objects**. When something goes wrong, a signal travels outward through the running code until a handler takes it — and if nothing does, the error prints and the program stops, exactly as it always did. Catching is opt-in: code that never mentions exceptions behaves as before.

## Signal and handle

```
[ 1/0 ] on: ZeroDivide do: [ :e | Transcript show: e messageText. 0 ]
```

The first block is the **protected block**; the second is the **handler**, and its argument `e` is the exception itself. If the protected block finishes, its value is the answer and the handler never runs. If it signals, the handler''s value is the answer instead — here `0`, after printing *division by zero*.

Raising your own is one message:

```ref
Error signal: ''the cupboard is bare''
```

`Object>>error:` funnels through the same machinery, so every library failure — a missing dictionary key, `first` on an empty collection — is catchable the same way.

## Catch by kind

Exceptions form a hierarchy, and a handler catches its class **and everything below it**:

- `Exception` — the root; a handler for it catches everything
- `Error` — anything that stops the computation
- `ZeroDivide` — division by zero
- `MessageNotUnderstood` — a message the receiver has no method for
- `IndexOutOfBounds` — an index outside the collection
- `Warning` — resumable; see below

So `on: Error do:` takes all four error kinds, while `on: ZeroDivide do:` lets a `MessageNotUnderstood` sail past to some outer handler. The VM''s own failures raise these same classes: `1/0`, `3 fooBar` and `#(1 2) at: 9` are all ordinary, catchable signals.

Each kind carries its evidence. Every exception answers `messageText`; a `MessageNotUnderstood` also answers `receiver` and `message selector`; an `IndexOutOfBounds` answers `index`.

## The verbs

Inside a handler, `e` understands four ways out:

- `e return: x` — answer `x` from the whole `on:do:`; the rest of the handler is abandoned
- `e retry` — run the protected block **again**, with a fresh handler armed
- `e pass` — decline: hand the exception to the next handler outward
- `e resume: x` — *resumable signals only*: go back and continue, with `x` as the value of `signal`

`retry` is a loop, not recursion — a handler that retries five thousand times grows no stack. `pass` searches strictly **outward**: an exception can never re-enter the handler that passed it.

## Warnings resume

An `Error` is not resumable — once raised, the computation below is abandoned. A `Warning` is: the signalling code is still there, waiting, and `resume:` continues it.

```
[ Transcript show: (Warning signal: ''low on tea'') printString ]
    on: Warning do: [ :w | w resume: #noted ]
```

Unhandled, a Warning prints its message and **carries on**, answering `nil` — a warning nobody is listening for should not kill the computation.

## ensure: always runs

```ref
[ self openValve. self pump ] ensure: [ self closeValve ]
```

The `ensure:` block runs however the protected block exits — normal return, an exception caught three levels out, even a handler''s `retry` (the abandoned attempt is cleaned up before the next begins). The order on a signal is the classic one: body, then handler, then the ensure blocks between them.

## Your own kinds

Subclass where it reads best, and give the class its default words:

```ref
Error subclass: OvenTooCold [
    description [ ^''the oven is not hot enough'' ]
]

[ self bake ] on: OvenTooCold do: [ :e | self preheat. e retry ]
```

The tests that pin all of this down are `world/tests/50`, `54` and `55` — worth a read; they are short and they show every verb in motion.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> stringsText [ ^'# Strings and Text

A macVM `String` is a **byte vector**: a sequence of bytes, one `Character` (0–255) per slot, and `size` counts the bytes. That is fast, simple, and exactly right — until text stops being ASCII. Then you have two honest tools: the UTF-8 helpers on String itself, and `CocoaString`, a second species that counts what Cocoa counts. Nothing guesses; each answers the question it is actually asked.

## The everyday verbs

```
| parts |
parts := ''1959,Mini,Issigonis'' splitOn: '',''.
Transcript show: ('' | '' join: parts); cr.
Transcript show: (''{2} designed the {1}'' format: (Array with: (parts at: 2) with: (parts at: 3))); cr
```

- `splitOn:` **keeps empty fields** — `''a,,b''` gives three pieces — which is what makes it right for records. `subStrings:` is the other convention: runs collapse, empties drop.
- `join:` is on the **separator**, and prints non-Strings for you: `'' + '' join: #(1 2 3)`.
- `format:` fills `{1}`, `{2}`… from an Array; `{{` and `}}` are literal braces; a slot with no argument is left visible rather than raising.
- `padLeftTo:` / `padRightTo:` (optionally `with: $0`) align and **never truncate**.
- `occurrencesOfString:` counts without overlap; `indexOfSubstring:startingAt:` walks matches; `startsWith:` / `endsWith:` / `beginsWith:` say what they mean.

## Bytes are not characters

```
''café'' size.          "5 — bytes"
''café'' utf8Size.      "4 — characters"
(''café'' codePointAt: 4). "233 — é"
''café'' codePoints asArray. "(99 97 102 233)"
```

`utf8Size`, `codePointAt:` and `codePoints` read the bytes **as UTF-8**. Malformed input answers `nil` — a byte-String legitimately may not be UTF-8, and a nil beats a guess. Ask `isValidUtf8` first when it matters, and take `asByteArray` when you really do want the bytes.

## CocoaString

`aString asCocoaString` wraps a real `NSString`. It speaks ordinary String protocol — `at:`, `copyFrom:to:`, `,`, comparison, `asUppercase` — but its unit is the **UTF-16 unit**, because that is NSString''s unit and AppKit''s:

```
''café'' asCocoaString size.            "4"
(''café'' asCocoaString at: 4).         "$é — the character, not half its bytes"
(''abcd'' asCocoaString rangeOf: ''bc''). "#(1 2) — 0-based, UTF-16, ready for AppKit"
''CAFÉ'' asCocoaString asLowercase.     "café — case beyond ASCII"
```

`characterCount` counts characters even where one character needs two UTF-16 units (the astral planes — emoji live there). `rangeOf:` answers `nil` for an absent substring — NSString''s NSNotFound is too big for a SmallInteger, so the honest miss is nil, not a sentinel. `CocoaString mutable:` gives an appendable one; `append:` grows it.

Conversion is where encoding questions belong, and it is **loud**: a String whose bytes are not valid UTF-8 raises at `asCocoaString` instead of being silently rewritten. `asString` comes back the other way, and either species is accepted anywhere the other is.

## Which to reach for

- protocol text, file bytes, speed — `String`
- counting characters, slicing user text — `String` plus the UTF-8 helpers
- anything headed into Cocoa: ranges, case, AppKit APIs — `CocoaString`

The pinned truth lives in `world/tests/56` and `57`: every number above is asserted there, from both sides of the disagreement.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> workersText [ ^'# Workers and VMs

macVM is a **multi-VM system**. The window you are looking at is drawn by one Smalltalk VM (the *interface* VM, on the main thread); your code runs in another (the *primary*); and either can spawn more — each with its **own heap, its own JIT, and its own OS thread**, up to thirty-two of them. The games in the Demos menu and the apps in the Tools menu each run in a VM of their own. On a machine with this many cores, parallel Smalltalk is just Smalltalk.

## No shared state — ever

Everything that crosses a VM boundary is a **deep copy**, carried as pickle bytes and rebuilt on the far side. There is no shared object, no lock, and no identity across heaps: you cannot race on what you cannot reach. What crosses is *data* — numbers, strings, arrays, whole object graphs. Blocks do not pickle, which is why messages carry event names and payloads rather than code — and classes never need to cross, because **every VM boots the same world**.

## Spawn, send, reply

```
| w |
w := Worker spawn: ''Worker onMessage: [ :m | Worker reply: m payload ]''.
w send: #(1 2 3) onReply: [ :r | Transcript showCr: ''echoed: '' , r printString ].
```

The spawn argument is an **init doit**, run once in the fresh VM — installing the `onMessage:` handler is what it is for. `send:` never waits: the sender carries on, and the reply arrives later as a **continuation**, matched by correlation id. Inside the worker, messages are dispatched strictly serially — one to completion before the next, so a handler never sees itself re-entered.

A handler receives a `WorkerMessage`: `payload` is the rebuilt copy, `from` says who sent it. `Worker reply:` answers the sender; `w send:` (no reply block) is fire-and-forget.

## Endings

Two ways out, and they are not the same:

- `w terminate` — an outside decision. The worker''s thread is asked to stop and its resources are reclaimed.
- `Worker exit: reason` — the VM ends **itself**, on purpose: teardown runs, `ensure:` blocks fire, a goodbye is announced. Closing an app window works this way — the display *asks* the app to leave, and the app owns its own ending.

A death, either kind, is delivered to the spawner as an ordinary message — `{#workerDied. id}` down the same inbox as everything else — and `Worker onWorkerDied:` hooks it. Supervisors are built from nothing more: the app host that relaunches a crashed tool is a dozen lines of this.

## Watching them

The **Monitor** tab is the roster: one row per live VM with its memory, GC and JIT counters. Spawn the echo worker above and watch it appear; right-click a row to ask a VM to exit. The transcript is also multi-VM aware — every VM writes to it directly, and worker lines arrive tagged with their id.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> monitorText [ ^'# The Monitor

The Monitor tab is the answer to one question, kept live: **what are all these VMs doing?** One table row per running VM — the primary, the interface VM, every spawned worker, app and demo — refreshed at a period you pick from the popup.

## The columns

- **VM / KIND / STATE** — who it is, what role it plays, and what it is doing now.
- **MEMORY / HEAP** — bytes in use against the heap''s size.
- **GC S·F** — collections so far: scavenges (the cheap, young-space ones) and full collections.
- **ALLOC/S** — allocation rate, derived here from the raw totals and the refresh period you chose. The busiest VM in the room is whoever''s number this is.
- **NMETHODS / COMPILES / DEOPTS / IC MISSES** — the JIT at work: how much compiled code exists, how fast it is being made, how often speculation had to be unwound, and how often a send site met a receiver it had not seen before.

## How to read it

A healthy program **warms up**: COMPILES climbs while the JIT meets your hot loops, then settles; DEOPTS ticks a little as it learns and then goes quiet. A DEOPTS column that keeps climbing means code is being re-profiled over and over — worth a look. ALLOC/S tells you who is generating garbage; GC S·F tells you what it costs. Scavenges are routine; frequent *full* collections are the number to respect.

## The bridge band

Below the table, the **UI BRIDGE** band counts the main-thread work no VM-side counter can see: drain passes, delegate callback dispatches, worker envelopes, script requests. When the interface feels busy, this is where busy is visible.

## Asking a VM to leave

Right-click a row and **Send exit** asks that VM to end itself — the polite form: its teardown runs, its `ensure:` blocks fire, and it announces its own ending. Nothing here kills. A VM that dies on its own stays on the roster for a minute — marked, so a crash is a fact you can see rather than a row that vanished — and is then buried, with anything it left behind audited.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> appGuideText [ ^'# Guide: Build an App

We are going to build a **temperature explorer** — twelve landmarks from absolute zero to the surface of the sun, with the name, the numbers, and a colour bar all following a slider — twice. First live in this window, then as a program running in a **VM of its own**. Both times the routine is the same, and it is the reactive one:

> STATE IN, WINDOW OUT. Your code holds some state and answers a description of the whole window — a spec. An event arrives, your handler changes the state, and the window is described again. You never touch a control: what is on screen is a function of the state, so the two cannot disagree. The differ is what makes this affordable — it compares the new spec to the old and patches only what changed.

The pieces are `AppSpec` (specs are pure data — no Cocoa in them anywhere) and `AppToolWindow` (which owns the native window, the controls, and their whole lifecycle — you never retain, wire, or free a view).

## 1. A window is data

A spec is a tree of typed nodes: containers (`stack:`, `row:`, `grid:columns:`, `card:`), controls (`heading:`, `text:`, `button:event:`, `slider:value:min:max:event:`, `input:value:event:`, `checkbox:checked:event:`, `select:value:options:event:`), and panes for pixels. Two rules do all the work:

- **Every node gets an id** — the last keyword of every constructor. The differ''s law is that identity is *(type, id)*: same identity with changed props patches in place; different identity rebuilds that subtree. Name everything and your window updates by tiny patches forever.
- **Handlers are not in the tree.** A control carries an event *name* (`#set`, `#reset`); the spec stays pure data — which is also exactly what lets it travel between VMs later.

## 2. Open it live

This block is a complete reactive app — twelve landmarks, a heading, the numbers in three scales, a painted colour bar, and a slider. The state is one index; the render block reads it; the handler writes it and asks for a rerender:

```ui
| ix stops |
stops := OrderedCollection new.
stops add: (Array with: ''Absolute zero'' with: -273);
      add: (Array with: ''Liquid nitrogen'' with: -196);
      add: (Array with: ''Dry ice'' with: -78);
      add: (Array with: ''Water freezes'' with: 0);
      add: (Array with: ''A warm room'' with: 20);
      add: (Array with: ''Your body'' with: 37);
      add: (Array with: ''A hot bath'' with: 45);
      add: (Array with: ''Water boils'' with: 100);
      add: (Array with: ''A pizza oven'' with: 450);
      add: (Array with: ''Flowing lava'' with: 1200);
      add: (Array with: ''Steel melts'' with: 1500);
      add: (Array with: ''Surface of the sun'' with: 5500).
ix := 6.
AppToolWindow register: #scale title: ''The Scale of Everything''
    width: 600 height: 300
    render: [ | s c |
        s := stops at: ix. c := s at: 2.
        AppSpec window: ''The Scale of Everything'' id: #scaleWin body: (
            AppSpec stack: (Array
                with: (AppSpec heading: (s at: 1) id: #name)
                with: (AppSpec text: c printString , '' C     '' ,
                        (c * 9 / 5 + 32) rounded printString , '' F     '' ,
                        (c + 273) printString , '' K'' id: #cfk)
                with: (AppSpec rgbaPane: #bar width: 552 height: 30)
                with: (AppSpec slider: ''Colder to hotter'' value: ix
                        min: 1 max: stops size event: #pick id: #dial))
                id: #col) ]
    handler: [ :e :v |
        e == #pick ifTrue: [ ix := (v rounded max: 1) min: stops size ].
        (AppToolWindow named: #scale) invalidatePane: #bar.
        (AppToolWindow named: #scale) rerender ]
    paint: [ :tool :paneId :region | | h w ph plane stride c r g b hot cold fill y x word |
        h := tool paneHandle: #bar.
        w := AppRender widthFor: h. ph := AppRender heightFor: h.
        plane := AppPixels planeFor: h width: w height: ph.
        plane isNil ifFalse: [
            stride := AppPixels strideFor: h.
            c := (stops at: ix) at: 2.
            c < 0
                ifTrue: [ r := 40. g := (90 + (c // 4)) max: 0. b := 255 ]
                ifFalse: [ c < 100
                    ifTrue: [ r := (60 + (c * 2)) min: 255. g := 170. b := 200 - c ]
                    ifFalse: [ c < 1500
                        ifTrue: [ r := 255. g := (60 + (c // 12)) min: 255. b := 40 ]
                        ifFalse: [ r := 255. g := 220. b := (120 + (c // 60)) min: 255 ] ] ].
            hot := AppPixels bgraR: r g: g b: b.
            cold := AppPixels bgraR: 58 g: 58 b: 64.
            fill := w * ix // stops size.
            y := 0.
            [ y < ph ] whileTrue: [
                x := 0.
                [ x < w ] whileTrue: [
                    word := x < fill ifTrue: [ hot ] ifFalse: [ cold ].
                    AppPixels at: plane x: x y: y stride: stride put: word.
                    x := x + 1 ].
                y := y + 1 ].
            AppRender present: h ] ].
AppToolWindow open: #scale.
```

Drag the slider from absolute zero to the sun. The name, the three temperature scales and the bar all follow — cold is blue, a warm room is green, lava is red, the sun is white-hot. Things worth noticing:

- The state is **one index in a closure**. No class, no instance variables — the whole window is a function of `ix`.
- The controls patch; the **bar is painted**. A spec places an `rgbaPane` and the `paint:` block fills it with pixels — `planeFor:` hands you the surface, `bgraR:g:b:` makes a pixel, `present:` shows it. Controls are the framework''s; pixels are yours. The handler says `invalidatePane:` because only it knows the bar''s contents changed — a diff of the spec never could.
- The Fahrenheit line says `rounded`, and it matters: `37 * 9 / 5 + 32` is *exact* Smalltalk arithmetic — the fraction `493/5` — until you decide how to show it. Landmarks read best as whole degrees.

Run the block again and you get a fresh registration and a fresh window; close the spare — close **hides** a tool window, so your state survives it.

## 3. Watch it patch, not rebuild

The framework counts its own work, and the counters are the proof of the id discipline:

```ui
Transcript showCr: ''patches: '' , AppToolWindow patchCount printString ,
    ''  rebuilds: '' , AppToolWindow rebuildCount printString.
(AppToolWindow named: #scale) dispatch: #pick value: 12.
Transcript showCr: ''patches: '' , AppToolWindow patchCount printString ,
    ''  rebuilds: '' , AppToolWindow rebuildCount printString.
```

`dispatch:value:` is exactly what a real slider drag delivers. Each one costs a few prop patches — `patches` grows, `rebuilds` stands still. Remove an `id:` from a node in step 2 and watch the difference: `normalize` stamps a positional auto-id on it, and the moment a sibling shifts, that subtree rebuilds instead of patching.

## 4. When your code is wrong

Three failures, three honest outcomes, none of them fatal:

- A render block that **raises** is reported to the transcript and the window keeps its last frame.
- A **malformed spec** is refused before it is half-realized, and the report names the tool.
- A handler that raises does not take the window down — fix it and carry on. The system stays live throughout; that is the whole point of a Smalltalk.

## 5. A program of its own

The **Tools** menu already runs apps this way: *Declarative Demo*, *Mixed Demo*, and the AppSpec sprite and sound editors are all reactive tools, and with **Debug ▸ Launch in own VM** on (the default), each one runs in a freshly spawned VM on its own thread and core:

```ui
CocoaUI launchApp: #demo tool: ''WinDemoTool'' panes: false.
```

Three VMs share the work, and each does one thing. The **primary** spawns the app VM — that is all. The **app VM** holds the model, the render block and the handler; it answers *frames* (whole specs, pickled across as real objects). The **interface VM** realizes frames and sends *events* back. The two ends talk directly. Your logic can loop, allocate, or crash without the window ever stuttering — and if the app VM dies, its window keeps the last frame while the transcript says so. Closing the window *asks the app to end*; it tears down, runs its `ensure:` blocks, and exits its VM.

A tool that can be launched this way is just a class that answers `spec` and takes `handle:value:` — the same two messages as step 2, so **a tool does not know or care which side of a VM boundary it is on**:

```ref
Object subclass: TempScale [
    <classVars: Ix>
    TempScale class >> ix [ ^Ix isNil ifTrue: [ 6 ] ifFalse: [ Ix ] ]
    TempScale class >> stops [ "...the twelve landmark Arrays from step 2..." ]
    TempScale class >> spec [
        ^AppSpec window: ''The Scale of Everything'' id: #scaleWin body: ( "...as step 2..." )
    ]
    TempScale class >> handle: event value: value [
        event == #pick ifTrue: [ Ix := (value rounded max: 1) min: self stops size ]
    ]
    TempScale class >> install [
        AppToolWindow register: #scale title: ''The Scale of Everything'' width: 600 height: 300
            render: [ TempScale spec ]
            handler: [ :e :v | TempScale handle: e value: v.
                               (AppToolWindow named: #scale) invalidatePane: #bar.
                               (AppToolWindow named: #scale) rerender ]
            paint: [ :tool :paneId :region | "...the bar, as step 2..." ]
    ]
]
```

One rule matters here: a spawned VM boots **the world**, not your Workspace — a class defined in a doit exists only in the VM that ran the doit. So a tool headed for its own VM lives in a world file: write it in the **Editor** tab and use **Add to World**, or drop the file in `world/` yourself, add it to `world.list`, and reseed (`./reseed-world.sh` — the app boots from the image, not from `.mst` files). Then `AppVmHost start: #scale tool: ''TempScale''` — or a Tools-menu entry — runs it in a VM of its own.

## 6. Pixels, and the escape hatch

When a window needs drawing, the spec *places* a pane (`rgbaPane:width:height:`, `indexedPane:`, `textGrid:columns:rows:`) and a `paint:` block fills it — pixels are yours, placement is the framework''s. The AppSpec sprite editor is built exactly this way; read `world/91b_winspriteed.mst`. And a spec can embed a hand-built native view with `foreignView:` — declarative, mixed, and fully-native are all allowed.

> One more thing worth knowing: AppSpec is the same contract as the Windows port''s WinSpec — alias classes make the names interchangeable — and the tools are portable BYTE-FOR-BYTE: the Declarative Demo you launched above is WinDemoTool, unedited from the sister repo.
' ]
    CocoaHelp class >> gameGuideText [ ^'# Guide: Build a Game

Press the play button beside a block and it runs. Every block that has one is a **complete program** — nothing is remembered between presses, so each step here shows the whole thing again, a little bigger. Copy the newest one and you always have something that works.

## 1. Make a noise

```
Sound coin play.
```

A whole working program. Try `Sound jump play` and `Sound zap play` — change the word, press play again.

## 2. Draw a picture

```
| pane |
pane := GamePane new.
pane cls: GamePane midnight.
pane disc: 250 y: 60 radius: 30 color: GamePane yellow.
pane fill: 0 y: 180 width: 320 height: 60 color: GamePane forest.
pane text: ''HELLO'' x: 120 y: 110 r: 255 g: 255 b: 255.
pane present.
```

A window opens with a sun over a night sky. **Escape** closes it. Change a number, press play again — the picture redraws.

The pane is **320 dots across and 240 down**, and (0, 0) is the **top-left** corner.

## 3. See what the engine can do

```launch
Breakout launch
```

A window opens and Breakout runs: bricks, a ball, a paddle on the arrow keys. **Escape** closes it. It is one ordinary class you can read in the Browser.

## 4. How a game really works

Two minutes, and it explains the thing that trips everyone up.

A game is a **program with a session**, not a handful of drawing commands. When you **launch** one, the app opens a game window and then calls your program about **60 times a second** to draw the next frame. Escape ends the session. One game runs at a time.

Every game has the same two halves:

- **`start`** — runs once. Make the pane, choose colours, set the starting positions, say what to do each frame with `onStep:`, then `run`.
- **`step`** — runs 60 times a second. Move things, draw the frame, `present` it.

`run` does not loop and does not block; it says *this program is live* and returns. The app owns the clock.

> A single PICTURE, like section 2, can be drawn straight from the Workspace — the first drawing command opens a window around itself. A GAME cannot: a frame loop has to keep running between frames, and a Workspace instruction is a one-off errand that finishes. So games live in a class and get LAUNCHED.

## 5. A ball that moves

Press play to teach the system this program:

```
Object subclass: MyGame [
    | pane x y dx dy paddle score |
    MyGame class >> launch [ ^self new start ]
    start [
        pane := GamePane new.
        x := 20. dx := 3.
        pane onStep: [ self step ].
        pane run
    ]
    step [
        x := x + dx.
        (x < 12 or: [ x > 308 ]) ifTrue: [ dx := 0 - dx ].
        pane cls: GamePane midnight.
        pane disc: x y: 120 radius: 12 color: GamePane yellow.
        pane present
    ]
]
```

The line `| pane x y dx dy paddle score |` names everything this game will ever remember — including the pieces later steps add. That is deliberate: **a class''s shape is fixed for its lifetime.** You can rewrite its methods as often as you like (that is what pressing play again does), but the list of instance variables is settled when the class is first defined, because live objects already have that shape and MACVM will not reshape them underneath you. So declare what you will need up front; an unused one is simply `nil`.

Nothing appears yet — you taught it, you did not start it. Now press play here:

```launch
MyGame launch
```

A yellow ball bounces across the window. **Escape** stops it.

## 6. Two rules that save an hour

**Colours come ready.** A new pane already holds sixteen colours, and each has a name: `GamePane black`, `midnight`, `plum`, `forest`, `brown`, `darkGrey`, `lightGrey`, `white`, `red`, `orange`, `yellow`, `green`, `blue`, `lavender`, `pink`, `peach`. Write `pane cls: GamePane midnight` and it just works.

Want a different look? Take a whole retro palette:

```ref
pane palette: #c64.        "Commodore 64"
pane palette: #cga.        "IBM CGA — early PC games"
pane palette: #spectrum.   "ZX Spectrum"
pane palette: #msx.        "MSX"
pane palette: #gameboy.    "four shades of green"
pane palette: #mono.       "sixteen greys"
```

Or mix your own: `pane paletteAt: 16 r: 20 g: 20 b: 40` sets slot 16 to a colour of your choosing (red, green and blue each 0 to 255). The slots you can set run **16 to 255** — below that belongs to the system, and slot 0 is see-through.

**Draw, then `present`.** Drawing happens on a hidden page; `present` is the moment it appears, like taking the photo. End every `step` with it.

**Teach, then launch.** Defining a class does not start it — that is what the launch block is for. (A one-off picture needs no launch; a running game always does.)

## 7. Add a paddle you can steer

The whole program again, with two new things: it remembers `paddle`, and it asks which arrow keys are held. Press play, then launch it below.

```
Object subclass: MyGame [
    | pane x y dx dy paddle score |
    MyGame class >> launch [ ^self new start ]
    start [
        pane := GamePane new.
        x := 160. y := 60. dx := 3. dy := 3. paddle := 140.
        pane onStep: [ self step ].
        pane run
    ]
    step [
        (pane keyHeld: GamePane keyLeft)  ifTrue: [ paddle := (paddle - 7) max: 0 ].
        (pane keyHeld: GamePane keyRight) ifTrue: [ paddle := (paddle + 7) min: 280 ].
        x := x + dx. y := y + dy.
        (x < 8 or: [ x > 312 ]) ifTrue: [ dx := 0 - dx ].
        y < 8 ifTrue: [ dy := 0 - dy ].
        (y > 210 and: [ y < 224 and: [ x > paddle and: [ x < (paddle + 40) ] ] ])
            ifTrue: [ dy := 0 - dy ].
        y > 240 ifTrue: [ x := 160. y := 60 ].
        pane cls: GamePane midnight.
        pane fill: paddle y: 216 width: 40 height: 6 color: GamePane blue.
        pane disc: x y: y radius: 6 color: GamePane yellow.
        pane present
    ]
]
```

```launch
MyGame launch
```

Left and right arrows move the paddle. Miss, and the ball starts again from the top.

## 8. Add sound and a score

Once more, whole. Two more lines in `step` — a `coin` when the paddle connects, and the score drawn on screen.

```
Object subclass: MyGame [
    | pane x y dx dy paddle score |
    MyGame class >> launch [ ^self new start ]
    start [
        pane := GamePane new.
        x := 160. y := 60. dx := 3. dy := 3. paddle := 140. score := 0.
        pane onStep: [ self step ].
        pane run
    ]
    step [
        (pane keyHeld: GamePane keyLeft)  ifTrue: [ paddle := (paddle - 7) max: 0 ].
        (pane keyHeld: GamePane keyRight) ifTrue: [ paddle := (paddle + 7) min: 280 ].
        x := x + dx. y := y + dy.
        (x < 8 or: [ x > 312 ]) ifTrue: [ dx := 0 - dx ].
        y < 8 ifTrue: [ dy := 0 - dy ].
        (y > 210 and: [ y < 224 and: [ x > paddle and: [ x < (paddle + 40) ] ] ])
            ifTrue: [ dy := 0 - dy. score := score + 1. Sound coin play ].
        y > 240 ifTrue: [ x := 160. y := 60. score := 0. Sound zap play ].
        pane cls: GamePane midnight.
        pane fill: paddle y: 216 width: 40 height: 6 color: GamePane blue.
        pane disc: x y: y radius: 6 color: GamePane yellow.
        pane text: ''SCORE '' , score printString x: 8 y: 8 r: 255 g: 255 b: 255.
        pane present
    ]
]
```

```launch
MyGame launch
```

That is a real little game: input, physics, collision, sound and a score. Everything after this is more of the same.

## 9. The screen, and what you can draw

The pane is **320 dots across and 240 down**, with (0, 0) at the **top-left**. These are the messages you send inside `start` and `step` — a vocabulary to borrow from, not a program to run:

```ref
pane cls: GamePane midnight.
pane point: 10 y: 10 color: GamePane white.
pane line: 0 y: 0 to: 319 y: 239 color: GamePane red.
pane fill: 40 y: 60 width: 240 height: 20 color: GamePane green.
pane disc: 160 y: 120 radius: 24 color: GamePane yellow.
pane text: ''SCORE 10'' x: 8 y: 8 r: 255 g: 255 b: 255.
pane present.
```

Keys, for `step`:

```ref
(pane keyHeld: GamePane keyLeft)  ifTrue: [ ... ].
(pane keyHeld: GamePane keyRight) ifTrue: [ ... ].
(pane keyHeld: GamePane keyUp)    ifTrue: [ ... ].
(pane keyHeld: GamePane keyDown)  ifTrue: [ ... ].
(pane keyHeld: GamePane keyA)     ifTrue: [ ... ].   "space"
(pane keyHeld: GamePane keyB)     ifTrue: [ ... ].   "Z"
```

Sounds, anywhere:

```ref
Sound coin play.
Sound jump play.
Sound zap play.
```

Sprites — small pictures that move without you repainting behind them. Each row is a line of pixels, each digit one of the sprite''s own colours:

```ref
| id |
id := pane defineSprite: #(''00111100'' ''01222210'' ''12222221'' ''01222210'' ''00111100'').
(Sprite pane: pane id: id) moveTo: 100 y: 80.
```

Cleanup, if your game starts anything that should stop with it:

```ref
pane onReset: [ ''stopped'' ]
```

**Tools - Sprite Editor** draws sprites for you; **Tools - Sound Editor** designs instruments.

## If nothing shows up

- Writing a **game**? Teaching the class is not starting it — press the launch block. (A single picture needs no launch.)
- Using a colour the pane knows? `GamePane red` and friends always work; raw numbers must be **16 or higher**.
- Did you `present` at the end of `step`?
- Did you press play on the **newest** whole program? Each block here replaces the last — nothing carries over between presses.
- Is another game still running? Escape closes it; only one runs at a time.

## Read the masters

Each is one readable class, in the Browser:

- `Breakout` — the same ideas as section 7, plus bricks and lives. The best next read.
- `Worms`, `Galaxigans` — sprites and swarms.
- `MandelZoom` — heavy arithmetic, the JIT at full stretch.

> Whole numbers are your friend: they behave predictably, and they run on the VM''s fastest path. Breakout''s physics is nothing but integer addition.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> assetEditorsText [ ^'# Guide: Edit Sprites and Sounds

The games in the **Demos** menu draw 16-colour sprites and play parametric sound effects — and both kinds of asset are **classes in the image**, editable like everything else. You could write them by hand. The two editors under **Tools** exist so you do not have to.

## The Sprite Editor

**Tools ▸ Sprite Editor…** opens a utility window: a big paint grid, the 16-colour palette with RGB sliders, and a frame strip.

- **Paint** by clicking and dragging on the grid. **Fill** floods a region; **Pick** lifts a colour off the grid into the palette selection. Colour 0 is transparent.
- **Frames** make animation: `<` and `>` step, **Add**/**Dup**/**Del** manage the strip, and the play switch runs the animation at the FPS you set — right in the editor.
- **Resize** changes the grid; the name field names the sprite.

**Preview** relaunches the current demo with your sheet in place, so you judge the sprite where it will actually live — at game scale, on the engine, in motion.

**Save** compiles your work into the image as a **sheet class** — a small class whose methods are the hex rows of each frame, marked `isSpriteSheet`. **Sheets** lists every sheet in the image; **Load** brings one back to the grid. **Copy Code** puts the class source on the clipboard instead, ready to paste into a world file.

## The Sound Editor

**Tools ▸ Sound Editor…** is an instrument panel over the engine''s parametric synth — the sfxr idea, wired to the real thing.

- Twelve **recipe sliders** shape the effect: envelope, pitch and sweep, and friends. The picture above them draws the envelope and sweep you have dialled.
- **Osc +** adds up to four oscillator rows — wave, frequency, level per row.
- **Play** auditions through the engine itself — the very synth the games use, so what you hear is what ships.
- **Preset** cycles the classic starting points; **Random** rolls a fresh effect; **Mutate** nudges the current one. Roll, listen, keep.

**Save**, **Load** and **Copy Code** work exactly as in the sprite editor — sound sheets are classes marked `isSoundSheet`.

## Where assets live

A sheet class is ordinary source **in the image**: it shows up in the Browser, survives with your world, and a demo uses it by name. That is the whole pipeline — no asset folders, no export step, no formats. Draw, save, and the class is already wherever the image goes.

> Both editors keep their state when closed — the window hides rather than dies, so reopening is instant and your work-in-progress is still on the grid.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> weatherGuideText [ ^'# Guide: A Little Weather App

The Sockets page taught the wire and Build an App taught reactive windows. This guide joins them: a real internet program — city in, forecast out — in one runnable block. It talks plain HTTP to `wttr.in`, a weather service that answers a single friendly line.

> This one needs the network. Without it, the app still opens and tells you honestly that the fetch failed.

## The whole app

```ui
| city report fetch |
city := ''London''.
report := ''Press Fetch.''.
fetch := [ :place |
    | crlf addrs v4 s rc req all mark i |
    crlf := (String with: (Character value: 13)) , (String with: (Character value: 10)).
    addrs := Dns blockingResolve: ''wttr.in''.
    v4 := addrs detect: [ :a | (a includesSubstring: '':'') not ] ifNone: [ nil ].
    v4 isNil
        ifTrue: [ ''no address — is the network up?'' ]
        ifFalse: [
            s := Socket domain: Posix afInet type: Posix sockStream protocol: 0.
            rc := s connectTo: (InetAddress fromString: v4) port: 80.
            rc < 0
                ifTrue: [ s close. ''connect failed (errno '' , Posix errno printString , '')'' ]
                ifFalse: [
                    req := ''GET /'' , place , ''?format=3 HTTP/1.1'' , crlf ,
                           ''Host: wttr.in'' , crlf , ''User-Agent: curl'' , crlf ,
                           ''Connection: close'' , crlf , crlf.
                    s send: req.
                    all := ''''.
                    [ | chunk | chunk := s receiveUpTo: 4096.
                      (chunk isNil or: [ chunk isEmpty ])
                          ifTrue: [ false ]
                          ifFalse: [ all := all , chunk. true ] ] whileTrue.
                    s close.
                    mark := crlf , crlf.
                    i := all indexOfSubstring: mark startingAt: 1.
                    i = 0 ifTrue: [ all ] ifFalse: [
                        (all copyFrom: i + 4 to: all size) trimSeparators ] ] ] ].
AppToolWindow register: #weather title: ''Weather''
    width: 460 height: 220
    render: [ AppSpec window: ''Weather'' id: #weatherWin body: (
        AppSpec stack: (Array
            with: (AppSpec heading: ''A little weather app'' id: #head)
            with: (AppSpec input: ''City'' value: city event: #city id: #where)
            with: (AppSpec button: ''Fetch'' event: #go id: #fetchBtn)
            with: (AppSpec text: report id: #out))
            id: #col) ]
    handler: [ :e :v |
        e == #city ifTrue: [ city := v ].
        e == #go ifTrue: [ report := fetch value: city ].
        (AppToolWindow named: #weather) rerender ].
AppToolWindow open: #weather.
```

Type a city, press **Fetch**, and the last line becomes something like `London: 🌤 +26°C` — pulled off the wire by the same `Socket`, `Dns` and `InetAddress` you met on the Sockets page, and put on screen by the same reactive loop you met in Build an App.

## What to notice

- **The whole HTTP client is visible.** Resolve the name, connect to port 80, write a request ending in a blank line, read until the connection closes, take what follows the header block. No library between you and the protocol — which is the point: when you can see the wire, you can speak anything over it.
- **Two events, one loop.** Typing fires `#city` with the field''s text; the button fires `#go`. Both just change state and rerender — the reactive routine does not care whether an event came from a keystroke or a network round-trip.
- **The fetch blocks, briefly.** This block runs in the interface VM, so the window holds its breath for the round-trip — fine for half a second. A grown-up version is exactly the Build an App step 5 move: put the tool in a class, run it in a VM of its own, and the interface never waits on the internet again.
- **The forecast''s little cloud** arrives as UTF-8 bytes in an ordinary byte-String and renders fine — the boundary rules from Strings and Text at work.
- It is plain HTTP on purpose: macVM speaks raw TCP, and `wttr.in` is one of the services that still answers it. TLS is a realizer-sized project for another day.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> testGuideText [ ^'# Guide: Test Your Code

Smalltalk invented unit testing. Kent Beck wrote **SUnit** for Smalltalk-80, and when the idea was carried to Java in 1997 it became JUnit — every *Unit since descends from that original. macVM ships the real thing, so if you have written a test in Pharo or Squeak, you already know this.

There is a **Tests** tab in the toolbar. It is the fastest way in.

## Your first test

A test class is a subclass of `TestCase`. Every method whose name begins with `test` **is** a test — found by reflection, so there is no list anywhere to keep in step.

```ref
TestCase subclass: PointTest [
    | origin |

    setUp    [ origin := 0 @ 0 ]
    tearDown [ origin := nil ]

    testAddingMovesBothAxes [
        self assert: (origin + (3 @ 4)) equals: 3 @ 4
    ]

    testDistanceIsPythagorean [
        self assert: (origin dist: 3 @ 4) closeTo: 5.0
    ]

    testOutOfRangeRaises [
        self should: [ (Array new: 3) at: 99 ] raise: Error
    ]
]
```

File that in, then run it straight from a Workspace:

```ref
PointTest run
```

**Every test gets its own fresh instance.** `setUp` runs before each one and `tearDown` after each one — including after a test that blows up. Nothing carries from one test to the next. That is what makes a suite worth trusting, so do not stash state in class variables hoping the next test will see it.

## What to assert

- `assert: aBoolean` — fails unless true
- `deny: aBoolean` — fails unless false
- `assert: actual equals: expected` — **prefer this one**
- `assert: actual closeTo: expected` — floats
- `should: aBlock raise: Error` — the block must raise
- `shouldnt: aBlock raise: Error` — the block must not

Any of them takes a `description:` as a final keyword if you want to say why in your own words.

Reach for `assert:equals:` over `assert: a = b` every time. The first reports *expected 7 but was 8*; the second only tells you a test broke, and then you go hunting.

## Failure is not error

macVM keeps these apart, and the difference matters:

- A **FAILURE** is an assertion that did not hold. Your code ran and gave the wrong answer.
- An **ERROR** is anything else — a `doesNotUnderstand`, a divide by zero, an index out of range. The test never got as far as judging anything.

A failure usually means your logic is wrong. An error usually means your test is wrong, or something underneath it broke.

## The Tests tab

Three panes. On the left, **SUITES**: every runnable suite, expandable to its tests, each with a dot carrying the last verdict — filled for passed, a cross for not, a faint dot for not yet run. On the right, **RESULTS**: one row per test with its status, time and failure message. Below that, **DETAIL**: the full text of whatever you selected.

**Run All** runs everything in the image. **Run Selected** runs the suite you picked, or the single test. Your tests run on the *persistent* VM behind the window, not the one drawing it, so the interface stays live while they go.

## From the command line

```ref
macvm-gui test
```

It prints a line per test and a summary, and exits 0 when green and 1 when not — so it drops straight into a build script or a git hook. Add `--repeat 2` to run everything twice in one image: the second pass is the first whose methods have been compiled by the JIT, which is the cheapest check that your code survives its own warm-up.

## Where your tests live

Write the file, then add one line to `world/tests.list`. That is the whole ceremony — suites are found by reflection, so there is no driver to update.

**One thing to know.** This window boots from the image, not from the `.mst` files on disk. After you edit anything under `world/`, reseed before you look here:

```ref
macvm-gui seed --world world
```

Otherwise the command line will show your new suite and the Tests tab will not — and the command line is the one that looks right.

## What ships

**666 tests across 73 suites**, green. Most of them test the library you are standing on: collections, streams, numbers, exceptions, the FFI, sockets, supervision. `SUnitTest` is SUnit testing itself — it runs deliberately-broken cases through a nested runner and checks the framework reports them correctly, because a test framework that mis-reports a failure is worse than none.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> automationText [ ^'# Automation

This environment is not a sealed application — it is live Smalltalk that answers from the outside as readily as from the Workspace. macVM ships an **AppleScript** dictionary, so any script — and any AI agent that can run `osascript` — can reach into the same running world your code already lives in. And the dictionary has grown past "run some code": a script can browse, debug, set breakpoints, and roll the whole world back.

## Evaluate, browse, look

Open the dictionary in Script Editor with *File ▸ Open Dictionary…* and choose macVM, then talk to the app by its bundle id:

```ref
tell application id "com.macvm.cocoa"
    evaluate "3 + 4"                       -- answers "7"
    set current view to canvas             -- switch tabs
    browse "OrderedCollection" selector "add:"
    snapshot in (POSIX file "/tmp/shot.png")
    get transcript                         -- read the log back
end tell
```

`evaluate` runs a string of Smalltalk and hands back the result as text; if the code raises, that becomes a real AppleScript error you can catch with `try … on error`. It accepts an optional `time limit` in seconds, because it runs on the supervisor beat rather than blocking the window — the interface never freezes waiting on your expression.

The properties are how a script *looks around*: `current view` (read-write — every tab name works: workspace, browser, browser2, outliner, editor, canvas, monitor, debugger, find, help), `appearance` (system, light, or dark), `workspace text` (read-write), `transcript` and `busy` (read-only), `transcript collapsed`, and the read-only selection trio `selected package`, `selected class`, `selected method` — whatever the Browser is pointed at right now.

## Debug from a script

The step debugger answers to scripting too. `debug` takes the same words the debugger''s own buttons mean, runs one, and answers the machine''s state — so a script (or an agent) can step and look, step and look:

```ref
tell application id "com.macvm.cocoa"
    set breakpoint "Breakout" selector "tick"
    evaluate "Breakout run" time limit 5
    debug "frame 2"
    debug "print ball"
    debug "step"
    get debugger state
    debug "continue"
    clear breakpoint "Breakout" selector "tick"
end tell
```

`debug` understands `step`, `next`, `finish`, `continue`, `abort`, `frame N`, and `print <expr>` — the whole vocabulary of the Debugger tab, minus the mouse.

## The world verbs

The world is a database, and the database verbs are scriptable — the same operations as Caring for Your World, callable from automation:

```ref
tell application id "com.macvm.cocoa"
    save snapshot                          -- answers the snapshot''s path
    evaluate "..."                         -- something ambitious
    revert last change                     -- undo the newest accepted method
    restore snapshot                       -- back to the newest snapshot
    compact world                          -- vacuum the database
    revert world                           -- all the way back to the shipped world
end tell
```

`restore snapshot` takes an optional `from (POSIX file …)`; without it, the newest snapshot wins. Together with `evaluate`, this is a transactional workflow: snapshot, experiment freely, and either keep the result or roll it back — from a script, with nothing touched by hand.

## From a shell, or an agent

Because AppleScript is reachable through `osascript`, macVM drives from a plain shell too — which is how a script, or an AI agent with a terminal, works the app without a hand on the mouse:

```ref
osascript -e ''tell application id "com.macvm.cocoa" to evaluate "3 factorial"''
```

The result prints straight to standard output. Point anything that can shell out — a Makefile, a test harness, an agent — at that one line and it is holding a conversation with the running image. (The first time, macOS asks once to allow the automation.)

## Why it matters

Because it is Smalltalk all the way down, *automating the app* and *programming the system* are the same act. A script does not poke at a frozen interface from outside; it sends messages into the identical live world the Workspace talks to — the same objects, the same classes, the same worker that runs your code. The window is one client of that world; a script, or an agent, is simply another. Anything you can do here by hand — evaluate, browse, debug, snapshot, revert — you can hand to something else to do for you.

> evaluate turns an error into an answer instead of a hang: a raise inside your string comes back as an AppleScript error the caller can catch, never a spinning beachball.
' ]
    CocoaHelp class >> worldCareText [ ^'# Caring for Your World

Your **world** is everything this system knows: every class, every method, the whole library and all the tools — including the browser you read it in. It is worth understanding where that lives, because keeping it safe is easy once you know, and bewildering if you don''t.

## Two homes, and only one of them is yours

The world has a **source** and a **live copy**.

The source is `world/*.mst` — ordinary text files, listed in `world/world.list`, loaded in order. That is where the system ships from.

The live copy is `world/image.sqlite3`, a **database**. It is built (*seeded*) from the source once, and from then on it is what the app actually boots. A freshly seeded one holds about **191 classes and 3,273 methods** in roughly **2.5 MB**.

This is the fact everything else follows from: **when you Accept a method in the Browser or the Editor, it goes into the database — not into the source files.** Your work is real, saved, and survives restarts; it just does not live in `world/*.mst` unless you deliberately put it there.

## Where your changes go

There are exactly two doors, and they behave very differently.

**Accept** (Browser or Editor) writes to the database. Every accept is a **new version**: the previous source is kept, and the last **100 versions** of each class and method stay on record. This is the safe, everyday door — nothing is overwritten, everything can be walked back.

**File ▸ Add to World** is the other door. It graduates a file into `world/user_<name>.mst`, adds it to `world.list`, and imports it — so the class survives even a full re-seed. That makes it permanent, and world files have **no version history and no undo**. Use it when something has earned its place, not while you are still experimenting. (It now refuses to add a class a core world file already defines, which would otherwise write a silent copy that shadows the original.)

**File ▸ File In** is deliberately temporary: it restarts from a clean world and loads your file on top. Nothing persists. That is the right tool for trying something out.

## The World menu

Everything you need to look after the world is in one place.

- **Save Snapshot** — writes the whole world, compressed, to `~/Documents/macVM Snapshots`. About **0.8 MB**, a second or two, and stamped so saves never overwrite each other. This is your seatbelt.
- **Restore Latest Snapshot…** — puts the newest snapshot back and reloads. The archive is checked before anything is replaced, so a wrong or damaged file is refused with your world untouched.
- **Revert to Previous Version and Reload** — walks the history back one step for the thing you edited most recently. Gentle: the older source returns *as a new version*, so the revert is itself revertable.
- **Compact World (drop history)…** — throws away every version but the current one and reclaims the space. Use it when the image has grown; the live code is untouched, but its history is gone for good.
- **Revert World to Original…** — deletes the database and re-seeds from `world/*.mst`. **Everything you accepted and never saved to a file is gone.** Classes you added with *Add to World* survive, because those are world files.

Each destructive item asks twice: the first click changes its title to say exactly what will be lost, the second does it. Nothing here happens by accident.

## How to work safely

**Snapshot before anything ambitious.** It costs a second and under a megabyte. Before a big refactor, before filing in someone else''s code, before an experiment you cannot describe in one sentence — take one.

**Prefer Accept while you explore.** It is versioned; you can always step back. Reach for *Add to World* only when a class is finished enough to keep.

**Compact when it grows, not by habit.** History is what makes the image large, and history is what makes revert possible. Snapshot first, then compact — that way the record still exists somewhere.

**You cannot revert past the beginning.** The system marks the moment the world was seeded, and *Revert to Previous Version* stops there. It will never walk back into the library''s own construction and hand you a half-built method. On an untouched world it simply says there is nothing to revert — which is the truth.

**A class''s shape is fixed once it exists.** You can rewrite its methods freely, but its instance variables are settled when it is first defined, because live objects already have that shape. Declare what you will need up front. Re-running an identical definition is fine — that is just a method update.

## Doing all of it from a script

Every one of these is scriptable, so a backup can be a cron job and an agent can look after its own world:

```ref
tell application id "com.macvm.cocoa"
    save snapshot                    -- answers the archive path
    compact world
    revert last change
    restore snapshot                 -- newest, or: from POSIX file "…"
    revert world
end tell
```

## The one idea worth keeping

Every other Smalltalk hands you an **image**: a frozen snapshot of a running heap, all of it, saved and reloaded as one opaque block. Lose it and you lose everything; corrupt it and there is little to say about what broke.

This world is not that. It is a **database** — rows for classes, rows for methods, each one versioned, queryable, revertable, exportable, and readable by tools that know nothing about Smalltalk. That is why you can walk a single method back through its history, prune the past without touching the present, archive the lot into a file you can email, and have every one of those operations be an ordinary transaction rather than an act of faith.

> The world is not a class Smalltalk image. It is a database — and that is what makes it safe to change.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> collectionsText [ ^'# Collections

Collections are where Smalltalk''s claim to readability is settled, and the trick is that there is one **enumeration protocol** over every kind. Learn eight messages and you can traverse, filter, and fold anything in the system — an Array, a Dictionary, a Set, a Bag — without writing a loop.

## The kinds

- `Array` — fixed size, literal syntax `#(1 2 3)`. The workhorse for data that does not grow.
- `OrderedCollection` — the growable default: `add:`, `addFirst:`, `removeLast`, and friends. When in doubt, this one.
- `Dictionary` — keyed: `at:put:`, `at:ifAbsent:`, `removeKey:`, `keysAndValuesDo:`, `keys`, `values`.
- `Set` — membership without duplicates, folding by **equality**. `IdentitySet` folds by identity instead: two equal-but-separate strings are one element of a Set and two of an IdentitySet.
- `IdentityDictionary` — the same distinction, keyed.
- `Bag` — counted membership: `add:` as many times as you like, `occurrencesOf:` answers how many.
- `SortedCollection` — keeps itself ordered as you add: `SortedCollection sortBlock: [ :a :b | a > b ]`.
- `Interval` — `1 to: 10 by: 3` is a real collection: `(1 to: 4) collect: [ :e | e * e ]` answers `#(1 4 9 16)` without materialising anything first.
- `ByteArray` — raw bytes, literal `#[104 105]`.
- `Association` — one `key -> value` pair; Dictionaries are made of them.

## The enumeration protocol

```
| words |
words := #(''the'' ''quick'' ''brown'' ''fox'') asOrderedCollection.
Transcript showCr: (words collect: [ :w | w asUppercase ]) printString.
Transcript showCr: (words select: [ :w | w size > 3 ]) printString.
Transcript showCr: (words detect: [ :w | w startsWith: ''q'' ] ifNone: [ ''none'' ]).
Transcript showCr: (words inject: 0 into: [ :sum :w | sum + w size ]) printString.
```

- `do:` — visit each element; `do:separatedBy:` runs a second block between them; `reverseDo:` walks backwards; `doWithIndex:` hands you the position too.
- `collect:` — transform each element into a new collection.
- `select:` / `reject:` — keep or drop by a predicate.
- `detect:ifNone:` — the first match, with an honest miss.
- `inject:into:` — the fold: thread an accumulator through.
- `count:`, `anySatisfy:`, `allSatisfy:`, `sum`, `max` — the questions you would otherwise write loops for.
- `with:do:` / `with:collect:` — walk two collections in lockstep.

All of it works on **every** kind — `aSet count: [ :e | e odd ]`, `aBag select: […]`, `aDictionary collect: […]` — because anything that only needs `do:` is defined once, at the top. One species note to know: `select:` and `collect:` on a Dictionary enumerate its **values**, and filtering answers a sequenceable result (an OrderedCollection), so chains keep working.

## Slicing and building

`copyFrom:to:`, `,` (concatenation), `copyWith:` (one longer), `copyWithout:` (drop every equal element), `reverse`, `indexOf:`, `includes:`, `first`, `last`, `isEmpty`, `notEmpty` — all present on the sequenceable kinds, and written against `at:`/`size` only, so `Interval` and `ByteArray` inherit them working.

## Sorting and converting

`asArray`, `asOrderedCollection`, `asSet`, `asBag`, `asSortedCollection:` cross between kinds; `sort` orders an OrderedCollection in place. Deduplicate by round-tripping through a Set; count by round-tripping through a Bag; order by handing a sort block to either sorter:

```
Transcript showCr: (#(3 1 2 1 3 1) asSet asSortedCollection: [ :a :b | a < b ]) asArray printString.
Transcript showCr: (#(3 1 2 1 3 1) asBag occurrencesOf: 1) printString.
```

## Removal is honest

`Dictionary>>removeKey:` and `Set>>remove:` really remove — no tombstones left behind — and the missing-element paths signal catchable errors, so `[ aSet anyOne ] on: Error do: […]` behaves. `at:ifAbsent:` and `detect:ifNone:` are the no-drama spellings when absence is an ordinary case.

> Streams are the other half of this story — ReadStream and WriteStream walk and build collections incrementally — and the Strings page covers the text-shaped members of the family.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> numbersText [ ^'# Numbers

macVM carries the full Smalltalk numeric tower, and its organising idea is **exactness**. Integers, fractions and scaled decimals are exact — arithmetic on them never loses a digit, however large it grows. Floats are the fast, approximate species you opt into. The tower coerces itself: mix any two kinds and the answer takes the more approximate side.

## The kinds

- `SmallInteger` — a tagged 61-bit integer, the everyday workhorse (`SmallInteger maxVal` is 2305843009213693951). Arithmetic that outgrows it **overflows automatically** into…
- `LargePositiveInteger` / `LargeNegativeInteger` — arbitrary precision. `40 factorial` and `2 raisedTo: 100` just work; nothing wraps, nothing saturates.
- `Fraction` — the exact quotient. `/` on integers answers one: `7/2` *is* seven halves, and it stays reduced — `(1/3) + (1/6)` answers `1/2`.
- `ScaledDecimal` — exact decimal with a fixed scale, written `1.50s2`. Money arithmetic without float dust: `1.50s2 + 0.25s2` is `1.75s2`, exactly.
- `Double` — the 64-bit IEEE float, written `0.1` or `1.0e10`. The approximate species, and the fastest: the JIT compiles Double loops to machine arithmetic.

Comparison works across the tower — `(3/2) < 1.6` is true, `1 = 1.0` is true — and `asFloat`, `asInteger`, `rounded`, `truncated`, `floor`, `ceiling` move values between kinds when you want the crossing to be explicit (`3.7 rounded` is 4; `3.7 truncated` is 3).

## Division, precisely

Smalltalk gives you every division and does not make you guess which one you got:

- `/` — the exact answer: `7/2` is a Fraction.
- `//` and `\\` — **floored** quotient and remainder: `-7 // 2` is -4, `-7 \\ 2` is 1 (the remainder takes the divisor''s sign).
- `quo:` and `rem:` — **truncated** toward zero: `-7 rem: 2` is -1.
- `gcd:`, `lcm:` — the integer classics.

## Bases and bits

Integer literals take any radix — `16rFF` is 255, `2r1010` is 10 — and print back the same way: `255 printString: 16` answers `''ff''`. The bit protocol is all there: `bitAnd:`, `bitOr:`, `bitXor:`, `bitShift:` (`1 bitShift: 10` is 1024), `bitAt:`, `highBit`, `isPowerOfTwo`.

## The transcendentals

`sqrt`, `sin`, `cos`, `tan`, `arcSin`, `arcTan:`, `exp`, `ln`, `log`, `raisedTo:` are defined on **every** Number — `2 sqrt` and `1 sin` work straight off an integer, coercing through Float exactly as you would by hand. `Double pi` is there (the float class is named Double, as in Strongtalk), with `degreesToRadians` / `radiansToDegrees` beside it, and integer-exact `sqrtFloor` when you want the root without leaving the integers.

## What to reach for

- counting, indexing, ids — `SmallInteger`, and let it overflow into Large when it must
- money, quantities that must not drift — `ScaledDecimal` or `Fraction`
- geometry, physics, signal work — `Double`, the JIT''s favourite
- number theory — Large integers and `gcd:`; exactness is the whole point

The pinned behaviour lives in the numeric tests of the world suite — and every number in this article was produced by the running system before it was written down.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> datesText [ ^'# Dates and Times

`Date today` and `Time now` answer this machine''s calendar and clock — zone-aware, daylight-saving included, read through the same C library the rest of the Mac trusts (`localtime_r`; the zone offset comes from the struct the OS fills in). Both classes are ordinary immutable values with ordinary protocol.

## Date

```
Date today.                              "August 15, 2026"
Date today dayOfWeek.                    "#Saturday"
Date newDay: 25 month: #December year: 2026.
```

- **Construction** — `today`, `todayUTC`, `newDay:month:year:` (month by name) or `newDay:monthIndex:year:`, and `fromEpochSeconds:` for anything that came from Unix time.
- **Accessors** — `year`, `month` (a Symbol like `#August`), `monthIndex`, `dayOfMonth`, `dayOfWeek` / `dayOfWeekIndex`, `dayOfYear`, `daysInMonth`, `isLeapYear`.
- **Comparison** — `<`, `<=`, `>`, `=` all work, so dates sort and compare directly.

## Date arithmetic

The honest unit is the **epoch day**: `asEpochDays` answers days since 1970, exact and timezone-free, and `fromEpochSeconds:` builds a Date back. Add days as integers in between:

```
| deadline |
deadline := Date fromEpochSeconds: (Date today asEpochDays + 30) * 86400.
deadline printString.
```

(Strongtalk''s own Date shipped its Julian-day arithmetic unfinished — `addDays:` was never implemented in the original — so macVM keeps the arithmetic where it is real: on the integers.)

## Time

```
Time now.                                "11:51:59"
Time now hour.
```

- `now` / `nowUTC` — the wall clock, zone-aware, as a Time holding seconds-since-midnight.
- `hour`, `minute`, `second` — the clock-face reading; `minutes` and `asSeconds` are the running totals.
- `fromSeconds:` builds one; Times compare with the same `<`, `<=`, `>`, `=` set.

## Measuring, not telling

Two clocks, two jobs — and mixing them up is the classic bug:

- `Time secondsSinceEpoch` is **calendar time**: Unix seconds, right for timestamps that leave the machine.
- `Time millisecondClockValue` is **elapsed time since the VM started**: right for measuring, wrong for telling time. The convenience is `Time millisecondsToRun: [ … ]`, which brackets a block with it and answers the cost.

> Everything on this page was executed before it was written: the dates you see are the day this article was generated, on the machine that generated it.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> socketsText [ ^'# Sockets

macVM speaks the full BSD socket surface — TCP, UDP, options, even unprivileged ICMP — as **pure Smalltalk over the FFI**. There is no C glue layer to read around: `Posix class >> sendFd:buf:len:flags:` *is* `send(2)`, one line per call, and everything above it is ordinary Smalltalk you can browse.

Two ways in, and they are both first-class: a **synchronous** path that reads like a script, and an **asynchronous** path that scales like a server.

## The synchronous path

For a tool, a probe, an agent with a job to do — straight-line code that blocks briefly and answers. `Socket` (TCP), `UdpSocket`, and `IcmpSocket` wrap an fd; `InetAddress` names the peer:

```
| a b reply |
a := UdpSocket boundToLoopback.
b := UdpSocket boundToLoopback.
b sendTo: InetAddress loopback port: a localPort data: ''ping''.
reply := a receive.
Transcript showCr: (reply at: 1).
a close. b close.
```

That is a complete two-socket exchange. The verbs: `connectTo:port:` and `send:` / `receiveUpTo:` for TCP; `sendTo:port:data:` and `receive` (answering data, sender, and port) for datagrams; `bindTo:port:`, `localPort`, `close`. Options are spelled out — `reuseAddress:`, `receiveBufferSize:`, TTL, `tcpNodelay` — and `Socket domain:type:protocol:` opens anything the kernel will give you.

**Errors are values here, not exceptions** — deliberately, because this is a syscall surface: a failed call answers a negative code and `Posix errno` says why, exactly as C would, with no unwinding mid-protocol. Connect to a closed port and you get `-1` and a readable errno, not a raise.

Name resolution is synchronous too: `Dns blockingResolve: ''localhost''` answers the addresses (here: `''::1''` and `''127.0.0.1''`).

## The asynchronous path

Servers should not block, so the second layer inverts the flow: an `IoWorker` pumps kernel readiness (kqueue), and an `IoStream` turns raw arrivals into the requests you actually mean — **continuation-based**, no blocking read anywhere:

```ref
listener := TcpListener on: iow backlog: 8.
listener onConnection: [ :stream |
    stream eachLineDo: [ :line |
        stream writeLine: ''echo: '' , line ] ].
```

- `nextLineDo:` — the next line, without its terminator; nil at end of input.
- `nextCount:do:` — exactly N bytes, however they arrived; `upToEndDo:` — everything, once the input ends.
- `eachLineDo:` — the server idiom: re-arms itself per line until the stream ends.
- `write:` / `writeLine:` — straight out the fd, answering the byte count actually written.

Requests queue strictly FIFO, each fires once when satisfiable, and a request registered inside a firing continuation joins the same sweep — the re-arm pattern is safe by construction. Writes go straight out the fd; in this version a write larger than the socket buffer can stall until the peer drains, so it is sized for replies, not bulk transfer.

## The floor you stand on

Memory handed to the kernel lives in a `NativeBuffer` — one mmap-backed page whose address never moves under GC, because a movable heap object must never meet a syscall. Two honest limits, both the platform''s: true raw sockets need root (the unprivileged `IcmpSocket` datagram path is how Ping works without it — see world/61d), and the FFI cannot call variadic C, so there is no `fcntl` non-blocking mode — readiness-with-amounts from kqueue does that job instead.

> The pinned behaviour lives in the socket and IoStream tests of the world suite; the synchronous example above ran, round-tripped its ping, and printed it before this page was written.
' ]

    CocoaHelp class >> internalsText [ ^'# Under the Hood

A short tour of the machine, for the curious. The deep versions live in `docs/` in the repository.

## Two engines, one truth

Code starts in a fast **bytecode interpreter**. Methods that prove hot are compiled by the **tier-1 JIT** to native arm64, *customized* to the receiver class — the Self trick — with inline caches that graduate mono to polymorphic to megamorphic as call sites get promiscuous.

The two engines are held to one law: across the entire 7,700-assertion world suite, interpreter output and JIT output must be **byte-identical**. Every compiler change runs that differential gate, plus GC stress (a scavenge before *every* allocation), plus deoptimization stress, plus the benchmark suite. What does not pay, does not land.

## Speculation and the way back

Compiled code speculates: it inlines the primitives the profile saw, fuses SmallInteger arithmetic, elides allocations. When a speculation fails, an **uncommon trap** fires and the VM *deoptimizes* — it rebuilds honest interpreter frames from compiler-recorded metadata, mid-execution, and carries on as if nothing happened. Loops can also enter compiled code mid-flight (**on-stack replacement**).

## Memory

A generational collector: new objects are born in **eden** and scavenged copying-style through survivor space; the old generation is compacted by a mark-slide **full GC**. Compiled frames carry oop maps so the collector can walk native stacks precisely — an area guarded by some of the project''s most paranoid stress gates.

## Many VMs, one screen

This is not one Smalltalk with threads bolted on. It is **many whole VMs** — each with its own heap, its own JIT, its own garbage collector, on its own OS thread — that share nothing and talk by passing deep copies of messages. `Worker spawn:` starts one from Smalltalk. Nothing is shared, so nothing needs a lock, and a VM that dies takes only itself.

Four kinds are usually running while you work. The **primary** holds your persistent world — the classes you edit and the objects you keep. The **UI VM** is pinned to the main thread and owns the glass: every window, every pixel, and nothing else. **App VMs** run tools built with the declarative framework — the sprite editor computes its own pixels in its own VM and ships the finished bytes to the display. **Demo VMs** run the games, one apiece, each beating at 60 Hz off the VM''s own timer service and reading the keyboard itself.

The point is felt rather than seen: a demo that hangs no longer hangs the environment, and a tool that crashes takes only its own VM. The window it left behind keeps its last frame instead of vanishing.

VMs message each other **directly** — a link is *learned*, not registered: an envelope carries the sender''s own inbox, so anyone who has messaged you is someone you can answer. An app talks to the display without the primary in the middle.

## How a VM ends

**A VM exits itself, and says so on the way out.** Closing a tool''s window, pressing Escape in a demo, or choosing *Send exit* from the Monitor''s right-click menu are all the same thing: a polite ask. The VM runs its own cleanup — closes the pane it opened, tears down its window — announces `#vmExiting` to everyone who cares, and then goes. Nothing is killed mid-thought.

Watch it happen in the **Monitor** tab: one row per VM, and a row that ends stays on screen as `dead` for a minute before it is swept, so you can see the transition rather than a row simply vanishing.

## The supervisor beat

Over all of that, the UI runs a **supervisor beat**: if a VM dies mid-request the supervisor recovers the in-flight state and reports the error — the window never goes down with the experiment. Scripted evaluation through AppleScript rides the same rails, with explicit error replies instead of a hung caller.

## The Cocoa bridge

Under the square brackets, Objective-C is **Smalltalk''s message-passing model bolted onto C** — Cox took the object model straight from Smalltalk-80. So Cocoa and this VM already agree on the one thing that matters: computation is objects sending each other messages. That makes the fit almost embarrassingly good. `Cocoa classNamed: ''NSWindow''` hands you the class as an ordinary object, and a keyword send simply *is* an Objective-C selector — `win makeKeyAndOrderFront: nil` means exactly what it reads as. The whole GUI — this browser, the editor, the debugger, this very page — is world code going through that one door.

Nothing is declared in advance. Any send an `ObjcRef` doesn''t answer itself drops into `doesNotUnderstand:`, which turns the message into a real `objc_msgSend`: arguments cross by **copy** (a Smalltalk `String` becomes a fresh `NSString`, numbers convert, small structs like `CGRect` ride across as short Arrays), the call runs, and the result comes back wrapped or converted. So `(Cocoa classNamed: ''NSCalendar'') currentCalendar` just works — the live runtime is the authority on what methods exist, not a hand-written interface file. Where the two worlds genuinely disagree is **memory**, and that part is worth being honest about.

### Two object worlds, two memory managers

They disagree about nearly everything a pointer cares about. This VM **moves** objects — a scavenge slides them between generations, compaction relocates them — and decides life by reachability. Cocoa **never moves** an object and decides life by a **retain count**: a number some other piece of code bumps and drops. Hand Cocoa a raw Smalltalk pointer and the next GC relocates it out from under Cocoa; stash a bare `id` in a Smalltalk object and it either leaks forever or gets released twice and corrupts a runtime we don''t own.

The rule that dissolves this is blunt: **the two collectors never see each other''s pointers.** A Cocoa reference lives in Smalltalk only inside an `ObjcRef`, which hides the raw `id` in its **byte tail** — the one region of an object the collector never scans. The GC is free to move the *wrapper*; Cocoa never moves the *target*; neither ever walks into the other''s graph. And the retain count earns its keep: it *is* the external root — Cocoa holding the object alive on our behalf, which is precisely what reference counting is for. The quiet headline of the whole design falls out of that: **the garbage collector is not modified at all.** No pin bits, no new roots, no scan hook — eight opaque bytes and a counter neither side shares.

### Who retains, who releases

Every `id` crossing *into* Smalltalk is **retained as it is wrapped**, so an `ObjcRef` always owns exactly one strong reference — which quietly defuses the classic autorelease-pool trap. Cocoa''s ownership rules are just conventions over selector names (`alloc`, `new`, `copy`, and `init` hand back something you already own), and the bridge mechanizes them the same way ARC''s compiler does, so `alloc init` never over-retains.

Giving a reference back is either scoped or explicit. `Cocoa poolDo: [:pool | ... ]` runs a block and releases every `ObjcRef` minted inside it — the ordinary "make some objects, use them, drop them" case with zero manual bookkeeping. Or `ref release` releases now and **poisons the wrapper**: every later send through it fails cleanly instead of dereferencing freed memory. The bias is deliberate — a reference dropped without release *leaks* its one count, which is recoverable and shows up in a wrap/release census (`MACVM_TRACE=cocoa`), whereas an over-release would corrupt a runtime we can''t debug. When in doubt, the bridge leaks.

### Callbacks and the main thread

Cocoa also calls *back* — a button''s action, a window''s delegate — and that direction never gets an oop either. The Smalltalk handler is registered under a **ticket**: a plain integer index into a GC-rooted registry. A small native trampoline carries only that ticket, and when the event fires it posts `{event. ticket. args}` through the same worker inbox the rest of the system uses. The handler then runs **between doits, on the VM thread**, so a callback never interrupts a send half-way — the strictly-serial world the game loop and the workers already rely on stays intact.

One last asymmetry: **AppKit is main-thread-only**, and the VM runs on its own thread. So a view or window message goes through `onMain` — `window onMain makeKeyAndOrderFront: nil` runs that one send on the main thread. Because main-to-VM traffic is always asynchronous (queued and drained on wake), VM-to-main is safe to do synchronously: no wait cycle can ever close, so there is no deadlock to design around.

## The game pane

Beside the tools is a **retro game pane**: a small, native, hardware-flavoured display for pixel-art games, built so a whole game is a page of Smalltalk. It is deliberately not a general graphics API — it gives you the handful of things an 8-bit machine gave you and nothing to get lost in.

Underneath it is a **command stream, not a canvas**. Each drawing method is a thin wrapper over a numbered game primitive, and the primitive touches no pixels — it posts a `GameCommand` onto the VM-to-GUI channel, and the GUI, on its main thread, applies it to a native Metal (`CAMetalLayer`) pane. The VM never sees the GPU; it speaks a tiny command language and the native side rasterizes. In a headless VM every command is a silent no-op, so game code is harmless to load anywhere.

### The palette

Colours are **indices, not RGB** — the retro constraint that makes the look. A pixel is a number 0..255 that the pane maps through a 256-entry palette to a real colour. **Index 0 is transparent**; your colours live at **16..255**, and a fresh `GamePane` already holds a usable 16-colour set in slots 16..31 (before that, a beginner''s first `color: 1` drew nothing onto a black window — the least friendly thing the engine did). Redefine one entry with `paletteAt: i r: g: b:`, or swap the whole set for a real hardware palette: `pane palette: #c64` (also `#cga #spectrum #msx #gameboy #mono #default`). A per-scanline palette override is there too, for copper-bar and gradient tricks.

### Drawing a frame

The verbs are the ones you''d expect and no more: `cls:` clears to a colour, then `point:y:color:`, `line:y:to:y:color:`, `fill:y:width:height:color:`, `disc:y:radius:color:`. For a whole CPU-generated frame — a software renderer, a Mandelbrot, a tilemap — `blit:` overwrites the entire pane from one `ByteArray` of `width*height` palette indices in a single command, far cheaper than a plot per pixel. Whatever you draw, **`present` is what shows it**: it is the one moment a frame becomes visible, and inside the loop you must end every frame with it. The pane never presents mid-frame on its own, precisely so a half-drawn frame is never seen.

### Sprites

Sprites are **retained, not redrawn**. `defineSprite:` takes a small grid of pixel art, places it, and hands back a `Sprite` you keep. It carries its own 16-colour palette (`colorAt: i r: g: b:`); you animate it by appending frames with `addFrame:` and selecting one, and you move it with `moveTo: x y:`. You never clear and repaint a sprite each tick — you move the handle and it reappears on the next presented frame. That is the split from the immediate drawing above: the background is repainted every frame, sprites persist between them.

### The loop

A game is a **step block, not a blocking loop**. `onStep:` registers a block; `run` starts a GUI-owned, main-thread frame timer that calls it once per tick (`frameRate:` sets the rate, default 60) and returns immediately, so the game runs while your doit does not hang. Inside the block, `keyHeld:` tests the live keyboard against named constants (`keyLeft`, `keyRight`, `keyA`, and friends). `onReset:` registers cleanup that fires however a session ends — Escape, the close button, a relaunch — so a game that spawned workers or native state always has a way to tidy up. **Breakout**, **MandelZoom**, **Worms** and **Galaxigans** are complete, readable examples: launch one from the Demos menu, then read its source in the browser.

## Pedigree, honestly measured

The benchmark protocol is fixed: identical workloads, microsecond clocks, interleaved runs on a quiet machine, medians reported. Against the fastest open Smalltalk VMs, MACVM wins the classic suite; against its other ancestor, the Dart VM, it trades blows and closes the gap release by release. The dashboards in this app run the very same code.

---

